        "installs": 1117635,
        "weeklyInstalls": [40968, 38034, 34332, 37782, 52357, 40522, 35702, 36026],
        "firstSeen": "2026-04-28"
      },
      "skillMd": {
        "version": "59db609eb1e18aaf59b63284d0e52255aff0c63c",
        "updated": "2026-10-28",
        "skew": 98
      },
      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Sets up a repo for the Matt Pocock skills: issue tracker, triage labels and domain docs.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "you're about to use Matt Pocock's engineering skills in a repo for the first time"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "asks where your issues live, which status labels to use and where the glossary and decision records go, then writes it all down"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "an \"Agent skills\" section in CLAUDE.md or AGENTS.md, pointing to config files under docs/agents/"
            },
            {
              "label": "Runs",
              "text": "only when you call it, normally once per repo"
            }
          ],
          "text": "The bundle's skills that file specs and tickets, or sort incoming issues, need to know where issues go and where the project's glossary and decision records (ADRs) live; this skill records it for them. The agent first looks around: the git remote, an existing CLAUDE.md or AGENTS.md, glossary and ADR folders, signs of a monorepo. Then it asks one question at a time, each with a recommended answer you can accept in a word. Where do issues live: GitHub (through the gh CLI), GitLab (through glab), Markdown files under .scratch/ in the repo, or something else you describe, like Jira or Linear? If the bundle's triage skill is installed: keep its five default status labels, such as needs-triage and ready-for-agent, or map them to labels you already use? Only in a monorepo does it offer a separate glossary per part of the codebase. It shows you drafts before writing, edits whichever of CLAUDE.md or AGENTS.md already exists, and creates missing labels on GitHub or GitLab. You can edit the files by hand later; rerun it only to switch trackers or start over.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/mattpocock/skills/tree/5beb35b040e3de97e54fa9e386d7e42d15c295c2/skills/engineering/setup-matt-pocock-skills"
        },
        "tags": [
          "category:skill-tools",
          "type:setup",
          "effect:local",
          "needs:none",
          "by:community",
          "issue-tracking",
          "docs-writing"
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "mattpocock/skills/skills/engineering/ask-matt",
      "name": "ask-matt",
      "skills.sh": {
        "url": "https://skills.sh/mattpocock/skills/ask-matt",
        "installs": 823484,
        "weeklyInstalls": [39031, 36017, 32320, 36399, 49795, 37789, 33162, 30020],
        "firstSeen": "2026-06-17"
      },
      "skillMd": {
        "version": "5beb35b040e3de97e54fa9e386d7e42d15c295c2",
        "updated": "2026-10-20",
        "skew": 0
      },
      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Picks the right skill or flow from the Matt Pocock bundle for your situation.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "you have Matt Pocock's skills installed and don't remember which one fits what you're doing"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "maps your situation onto one skill or a path through several, reading each skill before recommending it"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "a recommendation of which skills to run, in what order; you run them yourself"
            },
            {
              "label": "Also covers",
              "text": "between phases of work, whether to keep the session going, clear it, compact it, write a handoff or use a subagent"
            },
            {
              "label": "Runs",
              "text": "only when you call it"
            }
          ],
          "text": "A router: it knows how the bundle's skills fit together and points you to the right one. Most work follows one main path from idea to shipped code: an interview that sharpens the idea, an optional throwaway prototype, a spec split into tickets, each ticket built test-first and reviewed, then a retrospective that improves the agent's setup for next time. Other starting points join that path: sorting incoming issues, chasing a hard bug, or planning an effort too big for one session. It also places the standalone skills, such as research, teaching and handoffs, and the setup skill to run first. Between phases it walks a short decision tree: stay in the session if the next phase needs what's in it, clear it if nothing there matters, write a handoff only when the work moves to another tool, folder or person, send self-contained tasks to a subagent, and otherwise compact the conversation.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/mattpocock/skills/tree/5beb35b040e3de97e54fa9e386d7e42d15c295c2/skills/engineering/ask-matt"
        },
        "tags": [
          "category:skill-tools",
          "type:router",
          "effect:none",
          "needs:none",
          "by:community",
          "skill-discovery"
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "anthropics/skills/skills/skill-creator",
      "name": "skill-creator",
      "skills.sh": {
        "url": "https://skills.sh/anthropics/skills/skill-creator",
        "installs": 425751,
        "weeklyInstalls": [5922, 6261, 5820, 6399, 6513, 6124, 6146, 6097],
        "firstSeen": "2026-01-19"
      },
      "skillMd": {
        "version": "dbd4588f9e1033efb41dad4bef2f7947c8993d44",
        "updated": "2026-10-20",
        "skew": 0
      },
      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Creates, improves and eval-tests skills.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "you want to write a new skill, or find out whether an existing one actually helps"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "drafts the skill with you, runs test prompts with and without it, and lets you review the outputs side by side"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "a SKILL.md revised over rounds of tests, with a benchmark of pass rate, time and tokens"
            },
            {
              "label": "Also tunes",
              "text": "the description agents read to decide whether to load the skill, so it's picked up when it should be and not otherwise"
            },
            {
              "label": "Unlike",
              "text": "simpler skill-writing skills such as write-a-skill, it runs the skill on test prompts and measures the difference"
            },
            {
              "label": "Works best in",
              "text": "Claude Code; claude.ai skips the comparison runs, the benchmark and description tuning"
            }
          ],
          "text": "Anthropic's own skill for making skills. It starts wherever you are: an idea, a workflow you just did in the conversation (\"turn this into a skill\"), or a draft. It asks what the skill should do, when it should be used and what it should produce, then writes the SKILL.md. Next it writes two or three test prompts and runs each twice in parallel subagents, once with the skill and once without it, or with the old version. While they run it drafts pass-or-fail checks for the outputs, then grades them, totals pass rate, time and tokens into a benchmark, and opens a viewer in your browser where you click through each output and leave comments. It rewrites the skill from your feedback, preferring general fixes over ones tailored to the tests, and repeats until you're happy. Finally it can tune the description: you approve 20 sample requests, some that should load the skill and some that look similar but shouldn't, and a script tries up to five rewrites, keeping the one that scores best on requests it didn't learn from. To compare two versions, it can also have an agent judge their outputs without knowing which is which.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/anthropics/skills/tree/dbd4588f9e1033efb41dad4bef2f7947c8993d44/skills/skill-creator"
        },
        "tags": [
          "category:skill-tools",
          "type:workflow",
          "effect:local",
          "needs:subagents",
          "by:official",
          "skill-authoring",
          "testing",
          "agent-workflows"
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "mattpocock/skills/skills/productivity/writing-for-agents",
      "name": "writing-for-agents",
      "skills.sh": {
        "url": "https://skills.sh/mattpocock/skills/writing-for-agents",
        "installs": 527492,
        "weeklyInstalls": [39934, 37802, 33470, 33845, 44140, 37844, 34442, 31691],
        "firstSeen": "2026-07-27"
      },
      "skillMd": {
        "version": "5beb35b040e3de97e54fa9e386d7e42d15c295c2",
        "updated": "2026-10-20",
        "skew": 0
      },
      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Guides writing documents for agents: skills, AGENTS.md and CLAUDE.md.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "you're writing or editing a skill, an AGENTS.md or CLAUDE.md, or any doc an agent reads"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "gives the agent principles for writing docs that agents follow the same way every run"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "tighter agent docs: sharper descriptions, clear steps, reference moved into separate files, dead lines pruned"
            },
            {
              "label": "Runs",
              "text": "by itself whenever you create or edit a skill, AGENTS.md or CLAUDE.md"
            }
          ],
          "text": "Its goal is an agent that takes the same steps every run, even when the output differs. The lines an agent always sees, such as a skill's description or a line in AGENTS.md pointing to a doc, decide when it opens other material, so they should be sharply worded, with one trigger per case, and short, since they cost tokens every turn. Steps stay in the main file; reference that only some cases need moves to separate files. Each step should end on a condition the agent can check, so it doesn't stop early. Familiar words that carry a whole behaviour, like \"tight\" for a fast, reliable loop, beat spelling it out each time, and saying what to do works better than banning what not to do, since a ban puts the unwanted behaviour in mind. It prunes duplicates, stale lines, facts the agent can look up itself and instructions it would follow anyway. For skills, it also covers frontmatter and the choice between letting the agent start a skill, which costs tokens every turn, and leaving it to you, which means remembering it exists.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/mattpocock/skills/tree/5beb35b040e3de97e54fa9e386d7e42d15c295c2/skills/productivity/writing-for-agents"
        },
        "tags": [
          "category:skill-tools",
          "type:guidelines",
          "effect:none",
          "needs:none",
          "by:community",
          "skill-authoring",
          "docs-writing",
          "writing"
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "obra/superpowers/skills/using-superpowers",
      "name": "using-superpowers",
      "skills.sh": {
        "url": "https://skills.sh/obra/superpowers/using-superpowers",
        "installs": 281046,
        "weeklyInstalls": [5458, 5840, 5880, 5177, 4640, 5650, 5533, 5396],
        "firstSeen": "2026-01-19"
      },
      "skillMd": {
        "version": "bb92a77741419a4ab5f06e711a283343f1ada0c3",
        "updated": "2026-10-20",
        "skew": 0
      },
      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Makes the agent look for and invoke a matching skill before every response.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "you install the Superpowers bundle: it's the bundle's entry point"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "makes the agent check for a matching skill before every reply, even before a clarifying question"
            },
            {
              "label": "Priority",
              "text": "process skills such as brainstorming and systematic-debugging come before implementation skills"
            },
            {
              "label": "Needs",
              "text": "the rest of Superpowers to be useful"
            }
          ],
          "text": "It's the rule that makes the other Superpowers skills fire. Loaded at the start of a session, it tells the agent that if there's even a 1% chance a skill applies, it must load it before responding, including before asking a question or exploring the code, and announce which skill it's using. When several apply, process skills come first: brainstorming before building, systematic-debugging before fixing. It lists the excuses agents use to skip skills and answers each one, adapts to harnesses such as Claude Code and Codex, and gives way to your own instructions in CLAUDE.md or AGENTS.md. Subagents ignore it.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/obra/superpowers/tree/bb92a77741419a4ab5f06e711a283343f1ada0c3/skills/using-superpowers"
        },
        "tags": [
          "category:skill-tools",
          "type:router",
          "effect:none",
          "needs:none",
          "by:community",
          "skill-discovery",
          "agent-workflows"
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "obra/superpowers/skills/writing-skills",
      "name": "writing-skills",
      "skills.sh": {
        "url": "https://skills.sh/obra/superpowers/writing-skills",
        "installs": 228719,
        "weeklyInstalls": [5367, 5783, 5657, 4863, 4444, 6125, 6133, 5560],
        "firstSeen": "2026-01-19"
      },
      "skillMd": {
        "version": "2ce8aa15d05bd57213e6f746f953aafb7ded7a8b",
        "updated": "2026-10-22",
        "skew": 1577
      },
      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Writes skills test-first: pressure-test with subagents, then document.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "you're writing or editing a skill and want proof that agents actually follow it"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "tests a skill like code: run a subagent without it and watch it go wrong, then write the skill and check the subagent now behaves"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "a SKILL.md that answers the exact excuses agents gave in testing, with a description that only says when to use it"
            },
            {
              "label": "Unlike skill-creator",
              "text": "Anthropic's skill-creator scores a skill's outputs on test prompts; this checks whether agents obey it when tempted to cut corners"
            },
            {
              "label": "Needs",
              "text": "subagents, to run the tests"
            }
          ],
          "text": "Its rule is \"no skill without a failing test first\", for new skills and edits alike. The agent first gives a subagent a task that tempts it to break the rule the skill will teach, such as a scenario with a deadline, hours of work it would have to throw away, and the end of a long day. It runs this without the skill and notes every excuse the subagent gives, word for word. Then it writes the smallest skill that answers those excuses, reruns the tests until the subagent complies, and closes each new loophole it finds. Along the way it teaches how to write skills agents find and follow. The description says only when to use the skill, never its steps, because agents follow a summary instead of reading the body. Skills that load in every session stay under about 200 words. Rules agents skip under pressure get a table of excuses with answers; output that comes out in the wrong form gets a plain description of what to produce instead. Before full tests, new wording is tried on five or more fresh agents and compared with agents given no guidance.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/obra/superpowers/tree/bb92a77741419a4ab5f06e711a283343f1ada0c3/skills/writing-skills"
        },
        "tags": [
          "category:skill-tools",
          "type:workflow",
          "effect:local",
          "needs:subagents",
          "by:community",
          "skill-authoring",
          "testing"
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "browser-act/skills/browser-act-skill-forge",
      "name": "browser-act-skill-forge",
      "skills.sh": {
        "url": "https://skills.sh/browser-act/skills/browser-act-skill-forge",
        "installs": 85192,
        "weeklyInstalls": [13, 19, 17, 22, 15, 13, 12, 19],
        "firstSeen": "2026-05-14"
      },
      "skillMd": {
        "version": "11c057b03f92101642cadc9f840564574120d184",
        "updated": "2026-10-20",
        "skew": 0
      },
      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Turns a browser exploration of a website into a reusable skill.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "you'll pull data from, or act on, the same website again and again, or need hundreds or thousands of records from it"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "explores the site once in your browser, finds the data requests its pages make, or else reads the page, and builds a skill from that"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "a tested, installed skill per reusable step, such as search or a detail page: a SKILL.md plus Python scripts"
            },
            {
              "label": "Needs",
              "text": "the browser-act skill, and an API key from BrowserAct, a browser-automation service with a free tier"
            },
            {
              "label": "Unlike browser-act",
              "text": "which drives a browser for one task, it keeps what it learned as a skill you can rerun"
            }
          ],
          "text": "You say what data you want, or what action to perform, on which site; without a site, it searches for candidates and lets you choose. It splits the job into steps you'd reuse on their own, such as search or reading a detail page, confirms one plan with you, then works without further questions. For each step it explores the site in your logged-in browser, preferring the requests the site's own pages send to its servers over reading the page, and records exactly how to repeat them. It wraps each working piece in a Python script, writes a SKILL.md around it, has a subagent test it, and fixes and retests until it passes before installing it. If you asked for data now, such as 500 listings, it then runs the new skill in batches. It reads only what you could see yourself in that browser, and never uses third-party scrapers or the site's official developer API, so the result needs no extra keys. Recordings and results stay on your machine.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/browser-act/skills/tree/11c057b03f92101642cadc9f840564574120d184/browser-act-skill-forge"
        },
        "tags": [
          "category:skill-tools",
          "category:browser",
          "type:workflow",
          "effect:remote",
          "needs:cli",
          "needs:browser",
          "needs:account",
          "needs:subagents",
          "by:official",
          "stack:browser-act",
          "skill-authoring",
          "scraping",
          "browser-automation"
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "mattpocock/skills/skills/productivity/grill-me",
      "name": "grill-me",
      "skills.sh": {
        "url": "https://skills.sh/mattpocock/skills/grill-me",
        "installs": 1508200,
        "weeklyInstalls": [57027, 53625, 48139, 48386, 58939, 52386, 48664, 43368],
        "firstSeen": "2026-03-13"
      },
      "skillMd": {
        "version": "5beb35b040e3de97e54fa9e386d7e42d15c295c2",
        "updated": "2026-10-20",
        "skew": 0
      },
      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Interviews you relentlessly to sharpen a plan or design.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "you have a plan or design you haven't fully thought through"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "starts the grilling skill: rounds of numbered questions, each with a recommended answer"
            },
            {
              "label": "Needs",
              "text": "the grilling skill, from the same bundle"
            },
            {
              "label": "Runs",
              "text": "only when you call it"
            }
          ],
          "text": "A shortcut: its whole SKILL.md tells the agent to load the grilling skill, so once started the two behave the same. The difference is how they start. grill-me only runs when you call it by name, while the agent can pick up grilling by itself when you say you want your thinking stress-tested. During the session, the agent maps your plan as a tree of decisions and questions you round by round until nothing is left assumed.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/mattpocock/skills/tree/5beb35b040e3de97e54fa9e386d7e42d15c295c2/skills/productivity/grill-me"
        },
        "tags": [
          "category:planning",
          "type:workflow",
          "effect:none",
          "needs:none",
          "by:community",
          "interview",
          "planning",
          "requirements"
        ],
        "note": "Now a one-line alias that calls the grilling skill."
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "mattpocock/skills/skills/engineering/grill-with-docs",
      "name": "grill-with-docs",
      "skills.sh": {
        "url": "https://skills.sh/mattpocock/skills/grill-with-docs",
        "installs": 1291744,
        "weeklyInstalls": [49063, 46332, 41794, 41959, 52205, 44944, 41171, 37516],
        "firstSeen": "2026-04-28"
      },
      "skillMd": {
        "version": "5beb35b040e3de97e54fa9e386d7e42d15c295c2",
        "updated": "2026-10-20",
        "skew": 0
      },
      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Runs the same interview and writes ADRs and a glossary as it goes.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "you want a plan or design stress-tested in a repo, with the terms and decisions written down as you go"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "questions you in numbered rounds until nothing is left assumed, updating the glossary and decision records as answers settle"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "a sharpened plan, plus new entries in GLOSSARY.md and ADRs (short decision records) in the repo"
            },
            {
              "label": "Unlike grill-me",
              "text": "it leaves a paper trail; grill-me runs the same interview and saves nothing"
            },
            {
              "label": "Runs",
              "text": "only when you call it"
            }
          ],
          "text": "It combines two other skills from the same bundle. grilling runs the interview: it works through your plan one open decision at a time, asking numbered questions, each with a recommended answer, until every decision is settled, and it looks up facts in your files itself instead of asking you. domain-modeling runs alongside: it calls out words that clash with GLOSSARY.md or are vague (\"account\": the customer or the user?), invents edge cases, checks your claims against the code, adds each settled term to GLOSSARY.md right away, and offers an ADR only for a decision that is hard to reverse, surprising, and a real trade-off. It's the usual first step of the bundle's workflow when you're working in a repo.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/mattpocock/skills/tree/5beb35b040e3de97e54fa9e386d7e42d15c295c2/skills/engineering/grill-with-docs"
        },
        "tags": [
          "category:planning",
          "type:workflow",
          "effect:local",
          "needs:none",
          "by:community",
          "interview",
          "domain-modeling",
          "docs-writing"
        ],
        "note": "Calls the grilling and domain-modeling skills."
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "mattpocock/skills/skills/productivity/handoff",
      "name": "handoff",
      "skills.sh": {
        "url": "https://skills.sh/mattpocock/skills/handoff",
        "installs": 1105740,
        "weeklyInstalls": [43887, 41765, 37953, 38422, 54020, 43967, 39160, 35344],
        "firstSeen": "2026-05-06"
      },
      "skillMd": {
        "version": "5beb35b040e3de97e54fa9e386d7e42d15c295c2",
        "updated": "2026-10-20",
        "skew": 0
      },
      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Compacts the conversation into a handoff document for another agent.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "the work has to move to another tool, another folder, a colleague or a side session"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "summarizes the conversation into one Markdown file a fresh agent can continue from"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "a handoff file in your system's temp folder, ending with the skills the next agent should load"
            },
            {
              "label": "Unlike claude-handoff",
              "text": "which also starts a background Claude Code agent on the summary, this only writes the file; you start the next session"
            },
            {
              "label": "Runs",
              "text": "only when you call it, optionally with what the next session is for"
            }
          ],
          "text": "The agent writes up the current conversation so another agent can pick up the work. The file goes to the OS temp folder ($TMPDIR or /tmp, %TEMP% on Windows), not your project. It links to specs, plans, ADRs, issues, commits and diffs by path or URL instead of copying them, strips API keys, passwords and personal information, and lists the skills the next agent should load. If you say what the next session will focus on, the summary is tailored to it. To carry on in the same tool and folder, the bundle suggests compacting the conversation instead.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/mattpocock/skills/tree/5beb35b040e3de97e54fa9e386d7e42d15c295c2/skills/productivity/handoff"
        },
        "tags": [
          "category:planning",
          "type:command",
          "effect:local",
          "needs:none",
          "by:community",
          "handoff"
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "mattpocock/skills/skills/engineering/prototype",
      "name": "prototype",
      "skills.sh": {
        "url": "https://skills.sh/mattpocock/skills/prototype",
        "installs": 1048501,
        "weeklyInstalls": [40553, 38550, 34601, 35029, 44844, 38015, 34602, 32445],
        "firstSeen": "2026-05-06"
      },
      "skillMd": {
        "version": "5beb35b040e3de97e54fa9e386d7e42d15c295c2",
        "updated": "2026-10-20",
        "skew": 0
      },
      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Builds a throwaway prototype to answer a design question.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "a design question is hard to settle on paper: does this state model hold up, or what should this screen look like?"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "builds throwaway code shaped by the question: a clickable logic demo, or several UI variants to flip between"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "a prototype to try; once it has answered, the decision goes into the real code and the prototype onto a side branch"
            },
            {
              "label": "Runs",
              "text": "by itself when you ask to sanity-check some logic or explore what a UI should look like"
            }
          ],
          "text": "It first works out which kind of question it's answering. For logic, it writes one self-contained HTML file anyone can double-click, a non-developer included: the current state shown in plain domain words, a button for each action, and tabs of guided scenarios that walk through the happy path, a tricky edge case and something that should be impossible. The logic sits in a small pure module that can later move into the real code. For UI, it builds three to five structurally different versions, usually inside the existing page, switched with a ?variant= URL parameter and a floating bar that's hidden in production. Either way there are no tests, no saved data and no polish, and the full state is always on screen. Once the question is answered, the winning decision goes into the real code, the prototype is committed to a throwaway branch outside main, and the verdict is noted on the related issue or in a commit, pointing to that branch.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/mattpocock/skills/tree/5beb35b040e3de97e54fa9e386d7e42d15c295c2/skills/engineering/prototype"
        },
        "tags": [
          "category:planning",
          "type:workflow",
          "effect:local",
          "needs:none",
          "by:community",
          "prototyping"
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "mattpocock/skills/skills/productivity/grilling",
      "name": "grilling",
      "skills.sh": {
        "url": "https://skills.sh/mattpocock/skills/grilling",
        "installs": 1065186,
        "weeklyInstalls": [52487, 49789, 44480, 46132, 61775, 52706, 47202, 46289],
        "firstSeen": "2026-06-04"
      },
      "skillMd": {
        "version": "a496f5513d4a4cf889bc48e189e579f4b0bdf6aa",
        "updated": "2026-10-30",
        "skew": 5
      },
      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Stress-tests a plan, decision or idea, one round of questions at a time.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "you have a plan, design or decision you haven't fully thought through"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "asks every question it can in numbered rounds, each with its recommended answer"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "a shared understanding, with nothing left assumed, before any work starts"
            },
            {
              "label": "Also called",
              "text": "grill-me, a shortcut you call by name"
            }
          ],
          "text": "The agent treats your plan as a tree of decisions, where each decision opens further ones. Each round, it asks every question whose prerequisites are already settled: numbered, each with the answer it recommends, and worded so that \"yes\" accepts it. Your answers settle those branches and unlock the next round. Facts it can find in your files or tools, it looks up itself, with a subagent if needed, instead of asking you; the decisions stay yours. It ends when every branch has been visited, and doesn't act on the plan until you confirm you're aligned.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/mattpocock/skills/tree/5beb35b040e3de97e54fa9e386d7e42d15c295c2/skills/productivity/grilling"
        },
        "tags": [
          "category:planning",
          "type:workflow",
          "effect:none",
          "needs:none",
          "by:community",
          "interview",
          "planning",
          "requirements"
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "mattpocock/skills/skills/productivity/teach",
      "name": "teach",
      "skills.sh": {
        "url": "https://skills.sh/mattpocock/skills/teach",
        "installs": 921907,
        "weeklyInstalls": [40223, 37645, 33746, 33673, 47203, 39566, 35109, 31831],
        "firstSeen": "2026-05-27"
      },
      "skillMd": {
        "version": "5beb35b040e3de97e54fa9e386d7e42d15c295c2",
        "updated": "2026-10-20",
        "skew": 0
      },
      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Teaches you a skill or concept across sessions, inside the workspace.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "you want to learn a topic, code or not, over several sessions rather than get a one-off explanation"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "turns the current folder into a course: asks why you're learning, gathers trusted sources and writes short lessons"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "HTML lessons and printable reference sheets, plus files tracking your goal, sources and progress, all in the folder"
            },
            {
              "label": "Runs",
              "text": "only when you call it, with what you'd like to learn"
            }
          ],
          "text": "The folder holds the state of your learning, so each session picks up where the last one stopped. MISSION.md records why you want to learn the topic, and every lesson ties back to it; if that's unclear, the agent asks you first. RESOURCES.md lists trusted sources, since it's told not to trust its own memory, and lessons cite them. Each lesson is one short, well-designed HTML file that teaches one thing, pitched just beyond what you already know. It judges that from its learning records: one numbered note per insight you've gained, revised as you go. A lesson teaches what you need to know, then makes you practise with quizzes or real-world exercises that give quick feedback, asking you to recall from memory and spreading practice over time so it sticks. Each one recommends a primary source and links to reference sheets: cheat sheets, syntax, glossaries. For questions that need real-world experience, it points you to communities such as forums or local groups. It notes how you like to be taught in NOTES.md.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/mattpocock/skills/tree/5beb35b040e3de97e54fa9e386d7e42d15c295c2/skills/productivity/teach"
        },
        "tags": [
          "category:personal",
          "type:workflow",
          "effect:local",
          "needs:none",
          "by:community",
          "learning"
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "mattpocock/skills/skills/engineering/domain-modeling",
      "name": "domain-modeling",
      "skills.sh": {
        "url": "https://skills.sh/mattpocock/skills/domain-modeling",
        "installs": 945450,
        "weeklyInstalls": [45486, 43132, 38784, 43499, 53941, 43971, 39443, 36477],
        "firstSeen": "2026-06-04"
      },
      "skillMd": {
        "version": "5beb35b040e3de97e54fa9e386d7e42d15c295c2",
        "updated": "2026-10-20",
        "skew": 0
      },
      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Builds and sharpens the project's domain model, CONTEXT.md and ADRs.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "you're pinning down what your project's business terms mean, editing its GLOSSARY.md, or recording a design decision"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "challenges vague or conflicting terms, tests them with edge cases and checks them against the code"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "an up-to-date GLOSSARY.md and, for the big decisions only, ADRs (short decision records) in docs/adr/"
            },
            {
              "label": "Unlike grill-with-docs",
              "text": "it runs no interview of its own; it steps in whenever terms come up, and grill-with-docs runs it alongside its questions"
            },
            {
              "label": "Runs",
              "text": "by itself when terminology, the glossary or a decision record comes up"
            }
          ],
          "text": "It keeps the project's vocabulary straight: the words people and code use for the things the software deals with, like orders, customers or cancellations. It's for changing that vocabulary, not just looking it up. When you use a word differently from GLOSSARY.md, the agent says so at once; when a word is vague (\"account\": the customer or the user?), it proposes a precise one. It invents concrete scenarios that force you to say where one concept ends and another begins, and when you describe how something works, it checks the code and flags any contradiction. Each settled term goes into GLOSSARY.md immediately, and the glossary holds definitions only, no implementation details. It offers an ADR only when a decision is hard to reverse, would puzzle a future reader, and came from a real trade-off. Files are created only once there's something to write in them; a large repo can keep one glossary per area.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/mattpocock/skills/tree/5beb35b040e3de97e54fa9e386d7e42d15c295c2/skills/engineering/domain-modeling"
        },
        "tags": [
          "category:planning",
          "type:workflow",
          "effect:local",
          "needs:none",
          "by:community",
          "domain-modeling",
          "docs-writing"
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "mattpocock/skills/skills/engineering/codebase-design",
      "name": "codebase-design",
      "skills.sh": {
        "url": "https://skills.sh/mattpocock/skills/codebase-design",
        "installs": 921131,
        "weeklyInstalls": [43787, 41415, 37076, 43359, 52900, 42898, 37697, 38595],
        "firstSeen": "2026-06-04"
      },
      "skillMd": {
        "version": "5beb35b040e3de97e54fa9e386d7e42d15c295c2",
        "updated": "2026-10-20",
        "skew": 0
      },
      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Gives a shared vocabulary and principles for designing deep modules.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "you're designing or reshaping a module's interface, deciding where code can be swapped out, or making it easier to test"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "gives the agent fixed terms and rules for deep modules: a lot of behaviour behind an interface that's quick to learn"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "design discussions in one shared vocabulary, judged by how much callers get for what they must learn"
            },
            {
              "label": "Runs",
              "text": "by itself in design talk, or when another skill of the bundle needs the vocabulary"
            }
          ],
          "text": "The agent sticks to a small glossary instead of looser words like \"component\" or \"service\". A module is anything with an interface, from one function to a whole slice of the app. Its interface is everything a caller must know, including ordering rules, errors and performance, not just the type signature. A module is deep when callers get a lot of behaviour from an interface they can learn quickly, and shallow when the interface is nearly as complex as the code behind it. A seam is a place where behaviour can be swapped without editing the code there, and an adapter is what plugs in, such as a Postgres repository or an in-memory fake. Rules of thumb follow: if deleting a module would spread its complexity across its callers, it was earning its keep; tests go through the same interface callers use; add a seam only once two adapters actually need it. For testability: pass dependencies in, return results instead of changing things on the side. An extra file has several subagents each design a very different interface, to compare before choosing.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/mattpocock/skills/tree/5beb35b040e3de97e54fa9e386d7e42d15c295c2/skills/engineering/codebase-design"
        },
        "tags": [
          "category:planning",
          "type:guidelines",
          "effect:none",
          "needs:none",
          "by:community",
          "architecture"
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "mattpocock/skills/skills/engineering/wayfinder",
      "name": "wayfinder",
      "skills.sh": {
        "url": "https://skills.sh/mattpocock/skills/wayfinder",
        "installs": 781170,
        "weeklyInstalls": [40175, 37138, 33461, 33660, 47396, 39092, 34673, 34461],
        "firstSeen": "2026-07-01"
      },
      "skillMd": {
        "version": "a496f5513d4a4cf889bc48e189e579f4b0bdf6aa",
        "updated": "2026-10-30",
        "skew": 5
      },
      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Plans work too big for one session as a map of decision tickets.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "an effort is too big and unclear for one session, like a new project or a huge feature"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "lists the open decisions as tickets on your issue tracker, then settles one ticket per session"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "a parent issue recording every decision made, ready to turn into a spec: decisions, not code"
            },
            {
              "label": "Needs",
              "text": "an issue tracker set up by setup-matt-pocock-skills, or it falls back to local Markdown files"
            },
            {
              "label": "Unlike grill-with-docs",
              "text": "which sharpens an idea in one interview, it spreads the decisions over many sessions"
            },
            {
              "label": "Runs",
              "text": "only when you call it"
            }
          ],
          "text": "The first session asks you what the end point is: a spec, a decision, or a change such as a data migration. Then it questions you across the whole effort to find the decisions still open. It creates one parent issue as the map, listing the end point, decisions so far, questions still too vague to ticket, and what's out of scope. Each question sharp enough to state becomes a child ticket, sized for one session, linked by what blocks what, and typed: research (reading docs or APIs, done by subagents right away), prototype (something rough to react to), grilling (an interview with you) or task (manual work a decision waits on). Each later session takes the first unblocked ticket, assigns it to you so parallel sessions skip it, and resolves it, then posts the answer, closes the ticket, adds a line to the map, and turns questions that have become clear into new tickets. When nothing is left to decide, it stops; to-spec, from the same bundle, turns the result into a buildable spec.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/mattpocock/skills/tree/5beb35b040e3de97e54fa9e386d7e42d15c295c2/skills/engineering/wayfinder"
        },
        "tags": [
          "category:planning",
          "type:workflow",
          "effect:remote",
          "needs:issue-tracker",
          "needs:subagents",
          "by:community",
          "planning",
          "issue-tracking"
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "mattpocock/skills/skills/engineering/to-spec",
      "name": "to-spec",
      "skills.sh": {
        "url": "https://skills.sh/mattpocock/skills/to-spec",
        "installs": 776987,
        "weeklyInstalls": [42452, 39678, 35125, 35555, 45629, 38781, 34900, 32683],
        "firstSeen": "2026-07-02"
      },
      "skillMd": {
        "version": "5beb35b040e3de97e54fa9e386d7e42d15c295c2",
        "updated": "2026-10-20",
        "skew": 0
      },
      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Turns the conversation into a spec on the issue tracker.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "you've already talked a feature through and want it written up as a spec"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "turns the conversation and the code into a spec, without interviewing you, and files it on your issue tracker"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "an issue an agent can pick up directly: problem, solution, a long list of user stories, implementation and testing decisions, out of scope"
            },
            {
              "label": "Needs",
              "text": "an issue tracker set up by setup-matt-pocock-skills"
            },
            {
              "label": "Runs",
              "text": "only when you call it"
            }
          ],
          "text": "It writes up what you've already discussed rather than asking more. The agent reads the repo if it hasn't yet, and uses the project's glossary terms and recorded decisions. It checks one thing with you first: where in the code the feature's tests will plug in, preferring existing places and as few of them as possible. The spec then gives an extensive numbered list of user stories (\"As a…, I want…, so that…\"), the modules and interfaces to build or change, schema and API decisions, and what makes a good test here. It leaves out file paths and code, which go stale, except a snippet from an earlier prototype that pins down a decision better than prose. The issue is labelled ready-for-agent, so it skips triage. It usually follows an interview such as grill-with-docs, and comes before to-tickets, which splits the spec into tickets that agents build one at a time.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/mattpocock/skills/tree/5beb35b040e3de97e54fa9e386d7e42d15c295c2/skills/engineering/to-spec"
        },
        "tags": [
          "category:planning",
          "type:command",
          "effect:remote",
          "needs:issue-tracker",
          "by:community",
          "requirements",
          "issue-tracking"
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "mattpocock/skills/skills/engineering/to-tickets",
      "name": "to-tickets",
      "skills.sh": {
        "url": "https://skills.sh/mattpocock/skills/to-tickets",
        "installs": 773468,
        "weeklyInstalls": [41660, 38793, 34354, 34805, 49732, 40682, 35922, 33358],
        "firstSeen": "2026-07-02"
      },
      "skillMd": {
        "version": "5beb35b040e3de97e54fa9e386d7e42d15c295c2",
        "updated": "2026-10-20",
        "skew": 0
      },
      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Breaks a plan into tracer-bullet tickets with blocking edges.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "you have a plan or spec and want it split into tickets agents can build one at a time"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "slices the work into thin end-to-end tickets, each naming the tickets that must finish first, and checks the split with you"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "tickets on your tracker linked by \"blocked by\" relations, or one Markdown file per ticket in the repo"
            },
            {
              "label": "Needs",
              "text": "an issue tracker set up by setup-matt-pocock-skills"
            },
            {
              "label": "Runs",
              "text": "only when you call it"
            }
          ],
          "text": "It usually takes a spec from to-spec, which describes the whole feature, and cuts it into pieces. Each ticket is a tracer bullet: a narrow but complete path through every layer (schema, API, UI, tests) that works and can be checked on its own, and fits in one agent session started from scratch. Building one layer at a time is ruled out, and any refactoring that eases the work comes first. A wide refactor, such as renaming a column used everywhere, is split differently: add the new form beside the old, move callers over in batches, then delete the old form, so CI stays green throughout. Before publishing, it shows you the numbered list, with what each ticket delivers and what blocks it, and revises until you approve. Tickets go out blockers first, labelled ready-for-agent, with acceptance criteria and no file paths; on a local tracker they're numbered files under .scratch/. An agent can then take any ticket whose blockers are done.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/mattpocock/skills/tree/5beb35b040e3de97e54fa9e386d7e42d15c295c2/skills/engineering/to-tickets"
        },
        "tags": [
          "category:planning",
          "type:command",
          "effect:remote",
          "needs:issue-tracker",
          "by:community",
          "planning",
          "issue-tracking"
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "obra/superpowers/skills/brainstorming",
      "name": "brainstorming",
      "skills.sh": {
        "url": "https://skills.sh/obra/superpowers/brainstorming",
        "installs": 419875,
        "weeklyInstalls": [7891, 8462, 8129, 7166, 7033, 9334, 8941, 8117],
        "firstSeen": "2026-01-19"
      },
      "skillMd": {
        "version": "22f0b463e7a5e579904dd6211a520321035526f7",
        "updated": "2026-11-04",
        "skew": 1474
      },
      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Explores what you actually want before any creative work starts.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "you're about to build or plan something new, in software or not, and haven't spelled out what you want"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "asks one open question per message and plays back what it understood, part by part, until the picture is complete"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "a description of what to build that you approve: in chat for a small change, a design document for a project"
            },
            {
              "label": "Holds off",
              "text": "the agent builds nothing, not even scaffolding or installed packages, until you approve that description"
            },
            {
              "label": "Unlike grilling",
              "text": "Matt Pocock's grilling asks rounds of numbered questions with suggested answers; this one asks you to describe things in your own words"
            },
            {
              "label": "Can show",
              "text": "mockups, diagrams and clickable prototypes in a browser tab, if you accept its offer"
            }
          ],
          "text": "It assumes the agent knows less about what you want than it thinks. If your request already says everything (\"make the icon cornflower blue\"), it just does it. Otherwise its first message offers to skip the questions and asks about a real moment or a concrete picture. Each message then ends with a single question. It covers goals, non-goals, what would count as failure, things you've seen elsewhere, and which choices of tools you want to make yourself. It can offer to look through your code or the web, or build a throwaway prototype. As each part becomes clear, it describes it back in 200 to 300 words and asks what's wrong. It then sizes the work: a quick task gets done, a small change is described in chat, a project gets a written design document, committed to the repo. Before you see that document, a fresh subagent reviews it as the person who'd build from it, listing what it would still need to know; the agent settles what it can and brings you only the questions that are yours. For software, the next step is writing-plans, which turns the design into an implementation plan.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/obra/superpowers/tree/bb92a77741419a4ab5f06e711a283343f1ada0c3/skills/brainstorming"
        },
        "tags": [
          "category:planning",
          "type:workflow",
          "effect:local",
          "needs:none",
          "by:community",
          "requirements",
          "planning",
          "interview",
          "agent-workflows"
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "mattpocock/skills/skills/productivity/to-questionnaire",
      "name": "to-questionnaire",
      "skills.sh": {
        "url": "https://skills.sh/mattpocock/skills/to-questionnaire",
        "installs": 552414,
        "weeklyInstalls": [37179, 35563, 31444, 31576, 40802, 34949, 30800, 28664],
        "firstSeen": "2026-07-14"
      },
      "skillMd": {
        "version": "5beb35b040e3de97e54fa9e386d7e42d15c295c2",
        "updated": "2026-10-20",
        "skew": 0
      },
      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Turns a decision you can't answer alone into a questionnaire for someone else.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "a decision hinges on knowledge someone else has, and you need to get it out of them"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "asks you who the recipient is and what you need back, then writes the questions for them"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "a Markdown questionnaire in the current folder, to send for written answers or to fill in together in a meeting"
            },
            {
              "label": "Unlike grilling",
              "text": "that skill questions you about your own plan; this one asks you only about the recipient and what you need"
            },
            {
              "label": "Runs",
              "text": "only when you invoke it; the agent won't start it on its own"
            }
          ],
          "text": "It doesn't question you about the subject, since that's the part you can't answer. It asks two things, each in one go: who the questionnaire is for (their role, what they know, how they relate to you) and which decisions or facts you need back from them. The questions then aim at the gap between the two. It writes to-questionnaire-<topic>.md: the purpose and the decision riding on it, who it's from and to and how the answers will be used, one paragraph of context, a deadline and a note that \"I don't know\" is a useful answer, then the questions grouped by theme. They come most important first, since a written reply may come only once. Each asks one thing, leaves room for the answer right below, and adds a one-line \"why this matters\" where it could be misread. It ends with an \"anything else?\" catch-all, and the agent checks that everything you said you need is covered.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/mattpocock/skills/tree/5beb35b040e3de97e54fa9e386d7e42d15c295c2/skills/productivity/to-questionnaire"
        },
        "tags": [
          "category:planning",
          "type:command",
          "effect:local",
          "needs:none",
          "by:community",
          "requirements"
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "mattpocock/skills/skills/in-progress/loop-me",
      "name": "loop-me",
      "skills.sh": {
        "url": "https://skills.sh/mattpocock/skills/loop-me",
        "installs": 480686,
        "weeklyInstalls": [28843, 26963, 24164, 23906, 29880, 25821, 23304, 21406],
        "firstSeen": "2026-06-24"
      },
      "skillMd": {
        "version": "5beb35b040e3de97e54fa9e386d7e42d15c295c2",
        "updated": "2026-10-20",
        "skew": 0
      },
      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Grills you about the specs of workflows you want to build.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "you'd like to hand recurring work or routines to automation, but haven't pinned down how they should run"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "questions you round by round, each question with a recommended answer, until every routine is specified"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "one Markdown spec per workflow in workflows/, plus NOTES.md on your tools and your words for things"
            },
            {
              "label": "Done when",
              "text": "an agent could build the workflow without asking you a single question"
            },
            {
              "label": "Runs",
              "text": "only when you invoke it; it sits in its author's in-progress folder, so expect changes"
            }
          ],
          "text": "It calls each recurring pattern in your life a loop, from your week or your morning down to one repeated task, and the spec for running one a workflow. Name a workflow to design, or none and it goes looking, proposing loops you hadn't noticed. If NOTES.md is thin, it first asks about your world: the tools you use, the channels you process and your own terms for them. Then it questions you in rounds, each question with its recommended answer, and creates, edits or deletes files in workflows/ as answers land. It brings a few terms, used only when a workflow needs them: a trigger, what starts each run, either an event such as a new email or a schedule such as every morning; a checkpoint, where you verify or decide, pushed as late as possible so you're asked once, with everything prepared; a brief, the short, decision-ready summary you read there instead of the raw output. Nothing is assumed: a workflow only gets AI, a checkpoint or a schedule if your answers call for one. It writes specs only; building them is another agent's job.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/mattpocock/skills/tree/5beb35b040e3de97e54fa9e386d7e42d15c295c2/skills/in-progress/loop-me"
        },
        "tags": [
          "category:planning",
          "type:workflow",
          "effect:local",
          "needs:none",
          "by:community",
          "interview",
          "requirements"
        ]
      }
