      },
      "skillMd": {
        "version": "a496f5513d4a4cf889bc48e189e579f4b0bdf6aa",
        "updated": "2026-10-30",
        "skew": 5
      },
      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Scans a codebase for deepening opportunities, renders an HTML report, then grills through the one you pick.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "a codebase has grown hard to change, test or find your way around, and you want to know where to restructure first"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "finds places where thin, tangled modules could become one deep module (lots of behavior behind a small interface), then designs the one you pick with you"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "an HTML report of rated candidates with before/after diagrams, then a design worked out question by question"
            },
            {
              "label": "Needs",
              "text": "three skills by the same author: codebase-design for the design terms, grilling for the questioning, domain-modeling for the glossary and decision records"
            },
            {
              "label": "Runs",
              "text": "only when you invoke it; the agent won't start it on its own"
            }
          ],
          "text": "A deep module hides a lot of behavior behind a small interface; a shallow one has an interface nearly as complex as its code. This skill hunts for chances to deepen, for code that's easier to test and for agents to navigate. Unless you name an area, it starts where git history shows the most change. It reads the project's glossary and its architecture decision records (ADRs), then has a subagent explore and note friction: one idea spread over many small files, functions pulled out only so they can be tested, modules leaking into each other, untested code. For each suspect it asks whether deleting it would concentrate complexity or just move it. The candidates go into an HTML page in your temp folder, which it opens: per candidate, the files, the problem, the fix, the gains for tests, a before/after diagram and how strongly it recommends it, plus a top pick. It proposes no interfaces until you choose. Then it questions you through the design round by round, adds new terms to GLOSSARY.md, and offers to record an ADR when you reject a candidate for a reason future reviews should know.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/mattpocock/skills/tree/5beb35b040e3de97e54fa9e386d7e42d15c295c2/skills/engineering/improve-codebase-architecture"
        },
        "tags": [
          "category:engineering",
          "type:workflow",
          "effect:local",
          "needs:subagents",
          "by:community",
          "architecture",
          "refactoring"
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "mattpocock/skills/skills/engineering/tdd",
      "name": "tdd",
      "skills.sh": {
        "url": "https://skills.sh/mattpocock/skills/tdd",
        "installs": 1207695,
        "weeklyInstalls": [43678, 41124, 36998, 42084, 54986, 44593, 39153, 35932],
        "firstSeen": "2026-02-10"
      },
      "skillMd": {
        "version": "5beb35b040e3de97e54fa9e386d7e42d15c295c2",
        "updated": "2026-10-20",
        "skew": 0
      },
      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Guides test-first feature and bug work with a red-green-refactor loop.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "you want a feature or bug fix built test-first, or ask for red-green-refactor or integration tests"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "has you agree which public interfaces get tested, then loops: one failing test, then just enough code to pass it"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "code built in small slices, with tests that check behavior and survive refactoring"
            },
            {
              "label": "Unlike test-driven-development",
              "text": "the TDD skill in obra's Superpowers set refactors inside the loop; this one leaves refactoring for a later code review"
            },
            {
              "label": "Runs",
              "text": "by itself whenever you ask for test-first work"
            }
          ],
          "text": "Less a procedure than the rulebook for each red-green cycle: write a failing test (red), then make it pass (green). Before any test, the agent proposes which public interfaces to test at, the places where behavior can be observed without reaching inside, each with a note on what it catches and misses. You confirm them, and no test goes anywhere else. Then it works in vertical slices: one test, then the least code that passes it, then the next, each shaped by what the last one taught. Tests check what the code does through its interface, not how: no mocks of your own modules (only of outside systems such as payment APIs or the clock), no private methods, no checking the database behind the interface's back. It also bans tests that can't fail because they compute the expected value the same way the code does, and writing all the tests before any code. Test names follow the project's glossary if there is one.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/mattpocock/skills/tree/5beb35b040e3de97e54fa9e386d7e42d15c295c2/skills/engineering/tdd"
        },
        "tags": [
          "category:engineering",
          "type:workflow",
          "effect:local",
          "needs:none",
          "by:community",
          "tdd",
          "testing"
        ]
      }
    },
