    {
      "id": "mattpocock/skills/skills/in-progress/setup-ts-deep-modules",
      "name": "setup-ts-deep-modules",
      "skills.sh": {
        "url": "https://skills.sh/mattpocock/skills/setup-ts-deep-modules",
        "installs": 407928,
        "weeklyInstalls": [27915, 25969, 23066, 22668, 28434, 25170, 22748, 20889],
        "firstSeen": "2026-07-10"
      },
      "skillMd": {
        "version": "5beb35b040e3de97e54fa9e386d7e42d15c295c2",
        "updated": "2026-10-20",
        "skew": 0
      },
      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Enforces deep-module boundaries with dependency-cruiser.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "you want each package in a TypeScript repo used only through its entry files, enforced by a check"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "adds dependency-cruiser rules: outside code may import only a package's top-level files, never its subfolders, and imports may not form cycles"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "a lint:boundaries check run with your type check, an example package, a README, and a pointer from AGENTS.md or CLAUDE.md"
            },
            {
              "label": "Proves",
              "text": "the rules work, by adding a forbidden import and watching the check fail"
            },
            {
              "label": "Runs",
              "text": "only when you invoke it; it sits in its author's in-progress folder, so expect changes"
            }
          ],
          "text": "A deep module is a lot of behavior behind a small interface, and this skill makes that a rule a tool can enforce. Each folder under src/packages (or packages) is a package: its top-level files, such as index.ts or client.ts, are its public entry points, and everything in a subfolder, by convention lib/ for code and tests/ for tests, is private. dependency-cruiser, a tool that checks imports, then fails when outside code reaches into a subfolder or when imports form a cycle. Tests, even a package's own, must also go through its entry files, like any outside caller. Files inside a package still import each other freely, and several small entry files are preferred to one index that re-exports everything. The agent installs it with your package manager, merges into an existing config rather than overwriting it, adds the check to the command that already runs your type check, and leaves tsconfig alone. It's done only once the check passes, fails on a deliberate bad import, then passes again. It doesn't decide which packages may depend on which; that's left for you.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/mattpocock/skills/tree/5beb35b040e3de97e54fa9e386d7e42d15c295c2/skills/in-progress/setup-ts-deep-modules"
        },
        "tags": [
          "category:engineering",
          "type:setup",
