    {
      "id": "obra/superpowers/skills/test-driven-development",
      "name": "test-driven-development",
      "skills.sh": {
        "url": "https://skills.sh/obra/superpowers/test-driven-development",
        "installs": 271829,
        "weeklyInstalls": [6275, 6629, 6383, 6060, 5605, 7253, 6777, 6482],
        "firstSeen": "2026-01-19"
      },
      "skillMd": {
        "version": "bb92a77741419a4ab5f06e711a283343f1ada0c3",
        "updated": "2026-10-20",
        "skew": 0
      },
      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Writes the failing test first, then the code.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "the agent implements any feature, bug fix, refactor or behavior change"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "makes it write one failing test, watch it fail, write the least code that passes, then clean up"
            },
            {
              "label": "Forbids",
              "text": "code without a failing test first: code written before its test is deleted, not kept as a reference"
            },
            {
              "label": "Exceptions",
              "text": "throwaway prototypes, generated code and config files, each only with your permission"
            }
          ],
          "text": "Its one rule is \"no production code without a failing test first\". Each cycle starts with one small test of real behavior, with a clear name and no mocks unless they can't be avoided. The agent runs it and checks that it fails for the right reason: the feature is missing, not a typo. A test that passes straight away is testing existing behavior and gets fixed. Then it writes the simplest code that passes, with no extra options, and runs the project's whole test suite, not just the new test, reporting any failure it sees, even one it didn't cause. Only when everything passes does it refactor. A bug gets a test that reproduces it before any fix. The skill answers the usual excuses (too simple to test, I'll test after, I already tried it by hand, deleting hours of work is wasteful) and lists the signs that mean: delete the code and start over. A companion guide covers writing tests that fail when the code breaks, and when mocking is safe.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/obra/superpowers/tree/bb92a77741419a4ab5f06e711a283343f1ada0c3/skills/test-driven-development"
        },
        "tags": [
          "category:engineering",
          "type:workflow",
          "effect:local",
          "needs:none",
          "by:community",
          "tdd",
          "testing",
          "agent-workflows"
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "obra/superpowers/skills/executing-plans",
      "name": "executing-plans",
      "skills.sh": {
        "url": "https://skills.sh/obra/superpowers/executing-plans",
        "installs": 254796,
        "weeklyInstalls": [5983, 6180, 6071, 5796, 5334, 6977, 6652, 6016],
        "firstSeen": "2026-01-19"
      },
      "skillMd": {
        "version": "22f0b463e7a5e579904dd6211a520321035526f7",
        "updated": "2026-11-04",
        "skew": 372
      },
      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Executes an implementation plan inline in the current session.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "you have a plan from writing-plans and want the cheaper way to run it, or the agent can't start subagents"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "works through every task itself, test-first, on its own branch, without stopping to check in between tasks"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "tested commits, one fresh review of the whole branch, and a list of every decision it made for you"
            },
            {
              "label": "Unlike subagent-driven-development",
              "text": "which gives each task a fresh subagent and its own review; here the only review comes at the end"
            },
            {
              "label": "Needs",
              "text": "sibling skills: it runs scripts from subagent-driven-development and loads test-driven-development and verification-before-completion"
            }
          ],
          "text": "The agent reads the plan and its spec once, checks where tasks depend on each other's names and types, then takes the tasks in order. For each one it reads just that task's text, runs every step and compares the output with what the plan expects. When the code is wrong it debugs; when the plan is wrong it decides itself, with the spec as the authority. Each decision, and what it costs if wrong, goes into a progress file that also lets the agent pick up where it was after its context is compacted. It stops to ask only before something irreversible, security-sensitive or outside its branch, such as a merge or a push, or when the plan is too broken to guess. A script marks each task complete only if its tests pass. At the end a reviewer subagent on the strongest available model checks the whole branch, or the agent reviews it itself without subagents. It fixes serious findings in one pass, each with a test that failed first, lists its decisions and the minor issues it left, and hands over to finishing-a-development-branch, which offers to merge or open a PR.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/obra/superpowers/tree/bb92a77741419a4ab5f06e711a283343f1ada0c3/skills/executing-plans"
        },
        "tags": [
          "category:engineering",
          "type:workflow",
          "effect:local",
          "needs:none",
          "by:community",
          "implementation",
          "agent-workflows"
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "obra/superpowers/skills/verification-before-completion",
      "name": "verification-before-completion",
      "skills.sh": {
        "url": "https://skills.sh/obra/superpowers/verification-before-completion",
        "installs": 266618,
        "weeklyInstalls": [7118, 6983, 6740, 6749, 7799, 9884, 8571, 7960],
        "firstSeen": "2026-01-19"
      },
      "skillMd": {
        "version": "bb92a77741419a4ab5f06e711a283343f1ada0c3",
        "updated": "2026-10-20",
        "skew": 0
      },
      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Requires running verification commands before claiming work is done.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "the agent is about to say work is done, fixed or passing, or to commit, open a PR or move on"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "makes it run the command that proves the claim, read the full output, and only then report what it shows"
            },
            {
              "label": "Forbids",
              "text": "\"should work now\", \"looks correct\" and an early \"Done!\" or \"Perfect!\" without fresh evidence"
            },
            {
              "label": "Also covers",
              "text": "subagents: when one reports success, the agent checks the actual diff before believing it"
            }
          ],
          "text": "Its rule is \"no completion claims without fresh verification evidence\". Before any claim of success, or even a pleased \"Great!\", the agent names the command that would prove it, runs it in full, reads the output and exit code, and then states the result with the evidence, or the real status if it falls short. A run from earlier in the session doesn't count. It spells out what each claim needs: zero failures in the test output for \"tests pass\", a build that exits cleanly for \"build succeeds\" (a passing linter isn't enough), the original symptom retested for \"bug fixed\", and a line-by-line check against the plan for \"requirements met\". A new regression test only counts once it's been seen to fail with the fix undone. It lists the usual excuses (I'm confident, just this once, I'm tired), and tells the agent to treat words like \"should\" and \"probably\" as a sign to stop and run the check.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/obra/superpowers/tree/bb92a77741419a4ab5f06e711a283343f1ada0c3/skills/verification-before-completion"
        },
        "tags": [
          "category:engineering",
          "type:workflow",
          "effect:none",
          "needs:none",
          "by:community",
          "verification",
          "testing",
          "agent-workflows"
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "obra/superpowers/skills/subagent-driven-development",
      "name": "subagent-driven-development",
      "skills.sh": {
        "url": "https://skills.sh/obra/superpowers/subagent-driven-development",
        "installs": 249273,
        "weeklyInstalls": [6035, 6189, 5887, 5565, 5730, 8506, 6632, 5982],
        "firstSeen": "2026-01-19"
      },
      "skillMd": {
        "version": "22f0b463e7a5e579904dd6211a520321035526f7",
        "updated": "2026-11-04",
        "skew": 2344
      },
      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Executes a plan with one fresh subagent per task and a review after each.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "you have a plan with mostly independent tasks and want a review gate on every task"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "hands each task to a fresh implementer subagent, then has a reviewer subagent check it against the spec and for quality"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "reviewed commits per task, a final review of the whole branch, and a list of every decision made on your behalf"
            },
            {
              "label": "Unlike executing-plans",
              "text": "where the main agent does every task itself with one review at the end; this costs more but reviews each task before the next"
            },
            {
              "label": "Needs",
              "text": "an agent that can start subagents; it picks a cheaper model wherever a task allows"
            },
            {
              "label": "Runs",
              "text": "without pausing between tasks; it stops only for irreversible, security-sensitive or outside-the-branch actions"
            }
          ],
          "text": "The main agent stays a coordinator and never writes the code itself. It works on its own branch, reads the plan and spec, and checks the plan for tasks that contradict each other. Each implementer subagent gets only its task's text and the names and types it shares with other tasks; it writes the tests and code, commits and reports. A reviewer subagent then checks the diff, and findings go back for fixes, to a fresh implementer on a stronger model if the first one stays stuck. After five rounds the main agent decides what's left itself. Those decisions and any minor findings go into a progress file that also lets it pick up where it was after its context is compacted. The final review of the whole branch runs on the strongest available model; one subagent fixes its findings, and the agent hands over to finishing-a-development-branch, which offers to merge or open a PR.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/obra/superpowers/tree/bb92a77741419a4ab5f06e711a283343f1ada0c3/skills/subagent-driven-development"
        },
        "tags": [
          "category:engineering",
          "category:agents",
          "type:workflow",
          "effect:local",
          "needs:subagents",
          "by:community",
          "multi-agent",
          "implementation",
          "agent-workflows"
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "obra/superpowers/skills/dispatching-parallel-agents",
      "name": "dispatching-parallel-agents",
      "skills.sh": {
        "url": "https://skills.sh/obra/superpowers/dispatching-parallel-agents",
        "installs": 228760,
        "weeklyInstalls": [5679, 5711, 5292, 5179, 4721, 6152, 5699, 5258],
        "firstSeen": "2026-01-19"
      },
      "skillMd": {
        "version": "bb92a77741419a4ab5f06e711a283343f1ada0c3",
        "updated": "2026-10-20",
        "skew": 0
      },
      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Splits independent tasks across parallel agents.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "you have two or more problems that don't share state, such as test failures in unrelated files"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "gives each problem its own subagent with a focused, self-contained prompt, and starts them all at once"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "one summary per subagent, checked for conflicts and confirmed by a run of the full test suite"
            },
            {
              "label": "Not for",
              "text": "related failures, exploratory debugging, or subagents that would edit the same files"
            },
            {
              "label": "Unlike subagent-driven-development",
              "text": "which runs a written plan one task at a time; here there's no plan and the subagents work side by side"
            }
          ],
          "text": "When failures have different causes, investigating them one after another wastes time. The agent groups them by what's broken, such as one test file or subsystem each, and writes each subagent a prompt with a single scope, the error messages and test names, limits such as \"don't change production code\", and what to report back. It starts them all at once. When they return, it reads each summary, checks that no two touched the same code, runs the whole suite, and spot-checks the fixes, since several subagents can repeat the same kind of mistake. The skill walks through a real case: six failures across three files, fixed by three subagents with no conflicts.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/obra/superpowers/tree/bb92a77741419a4ab5f06e711a283343f1ada0c3/skills/dispatching-parallel-agents"
        },
        "tags": [
          "category:engineering",
          "category:agents",
          "type:workflow",
          "effect:local",
          "needs:subagents",
          "by:community",
          "multi-agent",
          "agent-workflows"
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "obra/superpowers/skills/using-git-worktrees",
      "name": "using-git-worktrees",
      "skills.sh": {
        "url": "https://skills.sh/obra/superpowers/using-git-worktrees",
        "installs": 227665,
        "weeklyInstalls": [5685, 5736, 5308, 4698, 4461, 6178, 5725, 5316],
        "firstSeen": "2026-01-19"
      },
      "skillMd": {
        "version": "bb92a77741419a4ab5f06e711a283343f1ada0c3",
        "updated": "2026-10-20",
        "skew": 0
      },
      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Isolates feature work in a git worktree.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "you're starting feature work or about to run a plan, and want your current branch left untouched"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "sets up a git worktree, a second checkout of the repo on its own branch, after asking you"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "that separate checkout, with dependencies installed and the tests passing as a baseline"
            },
            {
              "label": "Prefers",
              "text": "your coding agent's built-in worktree tool, when it has one, over running git worktree add itself"
            }
          ],
          "text": "Work in a worktree can't disturb the branch you're on. The agent first checks whether it's already in one, and if so uses it as is. Otherwise it asks whether you want one, unless your instructions already say. It uses your coding agent's own worktree tool when there is one, since the tool can't track a worktree made by hand. Failing that, it creates one under .worktrees/ at the project root (or worktrees/, or wherever your instructions say), after checking that the folder is git-ignored and committing a .gitignore change if not. If a sandbox blocks it, it says so and works in your current checkout. It then installs dependencies for Node, Rust, Python or Go and runs the tests; if they fail, it reports and asks before going on. Its counterpart, finishing-a-development-branch, merges or opens a PR at the end and removes the worktree.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/obra/superpowers/tree/bb92a77741419a4ab5f06e711a283343f1ada0c3/skills/using-git-worktrees"
        },
        "tags": [
          "category:engineering",
          "type:workflow",
          "effect:local",
          "needs:none",
          "by:community",
          "stack:git",
          "git",
          "agent-workflows"
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "obra/superpowers/skills/finishing-a-development-branch",
      "name": "finishing-a-development-branch",
      "skills.sh": {
        "url": "https://skills.sh/obra/superpowers/finishing-a-development-branch",
        "installs": 225057,
        "weeklyInstalls": [5651, 5681, 5259, 5189, 4773, 6219, 5701, 5296],
        "firstSeen": "2026-01-19"
      },
      "skillMd": {
        "version": "bb92a77741419a4ab5f06e711a283343f1ada0c3",
        "updated": "2026-10-20",
        "skew": 0
      },
      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Verifies tests, then merges, opens a PR or cleans up a finished branch.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "the work on a branch is done and you need to decide how to integrate it"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "runs the full test suite, then offers three choices: merge locally, push and open a PR, or keep the branch"
            },
            {
              "label": "Waits",
              "text": "for your choice: it never merges, pushes or deletes on its own"
            },
            {
              "label": "Discards",
              "text": "the work only if you ask for that yourself, and only after you type \"discard\""
            },
            {
              "label": "Cleans up",
              "text": "worktrees it created under .worktrees/ or worktrees/, never ones your coding agent created"
            }
          ],
          "text": "It's the last step after a plan has been carried out, for instance by executing-plans or subagent-driven-development. If the tests fail, it shows the failures and stops: the choices only come after a passing suite. It confirms which base branch to merge into, since merging into the wrong one is costly to undo. A local merge is followed by a test run on the merged result; if that fails, it stops with the branch and worktree intact. A PR is opened with the hosting service's CLI when there is one, following the repo's PR template, and the worktree is kept for review fixes. On a detached HEAD, merging isn't offered. When git refuses to remove a worktree because of uncommitted files, it shows them and asks whether to commit, move or delete them, never forcing the removal. It won't force-push after a rejected push unless you ask.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/obra/superpowers/tree/bb92a77741419a4ab5f06e711a283343f1ada0c3/skills/finishing-a-development-branch"
        },
        "tags": [
          "category:engineering",
          "type:workflow",
          "effect:remote",
          "needs:none",
          "by:community",
          "stack:git",
          "git",
          "agent-workflows"
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "mattpocock/skills/skills/engineering/implement-spec",
      "name": "implement-spec",
      "skills.sh": {
        "url": "https://skills.sh/mattpocock/skills/implement-spec",
        "installs": 339761,
        "weeklyInstalls": [28177, 26320, 23474, 29844, 42312, 35044, 31172, 30324],
        "firstSeen": "2026-08-21"
      },
      "skillMd": {
        "version": "a496f5513d4a4cf889bc48e189e579f4b0bdf6aa",
        "updated": "2026-10-30",
        "skew": 16
      },
      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Implements a spec through its tickets, with subagents.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "a spec has been split into tickets, and you want all of them built in parallel"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "puts a subagent on every ticket that's ready, each in its own git worktree, and merges their work as they finish"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "the whole spec on one integration branch, code-reviewed, with the tickets closed or a pull request ready for review"
            },
            {
              "label": "Unlike implement",
              "text": "that skill has one agent build one piece in your session; this runs many agents over every ticket of a spec"
            },
            {
              "label": "Needs",
              "text": "subagents, and an issue tracker configured with setup-matt-pocock-skills, the author's one-time repo setup"
            },
            {
              "label": "Runs",
              "text": "only when you invoke it; the agent won't start it on its own"
            }
          ],
          "text": "It starts from a spec and its tickets, such as the same author's to-spec and to-tickets skills write. It reads the tickets as a graph: a ticket can start once the tickets blocking it are done, so at any moment some set of them is ready. An optional exploration subagent first saves notes outside the repo for the others to read. Then it creates an integration branch, the one branch everything merges into. Each ready ticket gets a subagent in the background, in its own worktree and branch, building test-first. When one finishes, another subagent merges its work into the integration branch, and any tickets that unblocks are started. Messages between agents stay short and point to the spec, tickets, notes and commits instead of repeating them. Once every ticket is done, the branch gets a code review and one subagent fixes everything it raises. It ends by marking the draft pull request ready, if there is one, or closing the tickets the way your tracker does, then removes the worktrees.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/mattpocock/skills/tree/5beb35b040e3de97e54fa9e386d7e42d15c295c2/skills/engineering/implement-spec"
        },
        "tags": [
          "category:engineering",
          "type:workflow",
          "effect:remote",
          "needs:issue-tracker",
          "needs:subagents",
          "by:community",
          "implementation",
          "multi-agent",
          "issue-tracking"
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "wshobson/agents/plugins/javascript-typescript/skills/typescript-advanced-types",
      "name": "typescript-advanced-types",
      "skills.sh": {
        "url": "https://skills.sh/wshobson/agents/typescript-advanced-types",
        "installs": 97707,
        "weeklyInstalls": [3111, 2956, 3068, 2981, 2834, 3374, 3306, 3317],
