    {
      "id": "dietrichgebert/ponytail/skills/ponytail",
      "name": "ponytail",
      "skills.sh": {
        "url": "https://skills.sh/dietrichgebert/ponytail/ponytail",
        "installs": 121150,
        "weeklyInstalls": [4815, 4591, 4356, 5199, 10976, 14193, 9752, 9283],
        "firstSeen": "2026-06-12"
      },
      "skillMd": {
        "version": "09c434ab73ca43761c5d8b0c5d341926c2fba710",
        "updated": "2026-10-20",
        "skew": 0
      },
      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Forces the laziest solution that works: the best code is the code you never write.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "the agent over-engineers: abstractions, options, dependencies or code nobody asked for"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "makes the agent take the smallest change that fully solves the task, reusing what the codebase already has"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "short diffs, and replies that end with what it skipped or didn't check, and any risk"
            },
            {
              "label": "Levels",
              "text": "lite only suggests the smaller option, full applies the rules, ultra also questions any part of the request the need doesn't justify"
            },
            {
              "label": "Runs",
              "text": "for the whole session, until you say \"stop ponytail\" or \"normal mode\""
            },
            {
              "label": "Never cuts",
              "text": "validation of untrusted input, error handling that prevents data loss, security, accessibility, or anything you asked for"
            }
          ],
          "text": "It casts the agent as a lazy senior developer: the best code is the code never written. Before writing, the agent reads the code the task touches and lists every place the change must reach, such as callers, tests, fixtures and config. Then it takes the first option that fully works: skip what wasn't asked for and say so, reuse something already in the codebase, then the standard library, then an installed dependency, never a new dependency for a few lines, and only then write the minimum new code. It's lazy about the solution, not the finish: it still updates every caller and test the change breaks, fixes a bug once at its root, adds one small test for non-trivial logic, and leaves a comment on any known shortcut, naming its limit and when to replace it. Where caveman, a skill for terse replies, trims the agent's words, ponytail trims its code.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/dietrichgebert/ponytail/tree/09c434ab73ca43761c5d8b0c5d341926c2fba710/skills/ponytail"
        },
        "tags": [
          "category:engineering",
          "type:mode",
          "effect:none",
          "needs:none",
          "by:community",
          "scope-control",
          "code-quality"
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "juliusbrussee/caveman/skills/safe-refactor",
      "name": "safe-refactor",
      "skills.sh": {
        "url": "https://skills.sh/juliusbrussee/caveman/safe-refactor",
        "installs": 118728,
        "weeklyInstalls": [12518, 13123, 10908, 11006, 8448, 7239, 7648, 7234],
        "firstSeen": "2026-08-11"
      },
      "skillMd": {
        "version": "bad48a0e1df55e05e3f52249afa35ecc1909ee76",
        "updated": "2026-10-20",
        "skew": 0
      },
      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Restructures code while preserving behavior, with verification around each edit.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "you're restructuring code, by extracting, merging or moving it, and its behavior must stay exactly the same"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "sets up a check of current behavior before editing, keeps feature changes out, and moves one piece of responsibility at a time"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "restructured code that passes the same checks as before, with every intermediate step still building"
            },
            {
              "label": "Unlike migration",
              "text": "which moves data, schemas or APIs with a rollback plan, this changes only how the code is organized"
            },
            {
              "label": "Runs",
              "text": "on its own, whenever the agent sees a task that fits; it's a short list of rules, not a step-by-step guide"
            }
          ],
          "text": "Before any structural edit, the agent defines which behavior must be preserved and how it will verify that. It keeps new features out of the refactor, moves responsibility from one part of the code to another one step at a time, and preserves public interfaces, error behavior, ordering and compatibility unless you've said otherwise. Every intermediate state must still build and pass tests, and it adds no dependency or configuration unless correctness needs it. At the end it runs the same verification again, and stops once behavior matches and the requested structure is in place.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/juliusbrussee/caveman/tree/bad48a0e1df55e05e3f52249afa35ecc1909ee76/skills/safe-refactor"
        },
        "tags": [
          "category:engineering",
          "type:workflow",
          "effect:local",
          "needs:none",
          "by:community",
          "refactoring",
          "verification"
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "juliusbrussee/caveman/skills/surgical-patch",
      "name": "surgical-patch",
      "skills.sh": {
        "url": "https://skills.sh/juliusbrussee/caveman/surgical-patch",
        "installs": 116953,
        "weeklyInstalls": [12457, 13066, 10850, 10087, 8239, 7093, 7564, 7185],
        "firstSeen": "2026-08-11"
      },
      "skillMd": {
        "version": "bad48a0e1df55e05e3f52249afa35ecc1909ee76",
        "updated": "2026-10-20",
        "skew": 0
      },
      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Fixes bugs at the narrowest layer that owns the wrong behavior.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "you're fixing a bug or making a small behavior change, and nothing else in the code should move"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "reproduces the failure when that's cheap, traces it to its cause, and changes only the smallest part of the code responsible"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "a minimal fix with a regression test, and no cleanup, renaming or new abstractions"
            },
            {
              "label": "Unlike investigate-first",
              "text": "which stops at a diagnosis, this makes the fix and proves it"
            },
            {
              "label": "Runs",
              "text": "on its own, whenever the agent sees a task that fits; it's a short list of rules, not a step-by-step guide"
            }
          ],
          "text": "The agent reproduces the failure first if that's cheap, otherwise gathers the strongest evidence it can. It traces the symptom to what actually causes it and fixes the code there, as narrowly as possible, rather than patching wherever the symptom shows up. It leaves unrelated behavior and your own edits alone, skips any cleanup, renaming or abstraction beyond the fix, and adds only the regression test the task needs. It then runs that test plus the nearest wider check the change affects, and stops once the failure is fixed and the test passes.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/juliusbrussee/caveman/tree/bad48a0e1df55e05e3f52249afa35ecc1909ee76/skills/surgical-patch"
        },
        "tags": [
          "category:engineering",
          "type:workflow",
          "effect:local",
          "needs:none",
          "by:community",
          "debugging",
          "scope-control"
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "juliusbrussee/caveman/skills/lean-build",
      "name": "lean-build",
      "skills.sh": {
        "url": "https://skills.sh/juliusbrussee/caveman/lean-build",
        "installs": 116620,
        "weeklyInstalls": [12381, 13034, 10820, 10020, 8278, 7152, 7532, 7165],
        "firstSeen": "2026-08-11"
      },
      "skillMd": {
        "version": "bad48a0e1df55e05e3f52249afa35ecc1909ee76",
        "updated": "2026-10-20",
        "skew": 0
      },
      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Builds features with strict scope to avoid overbuilding.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "you're adding a feature or an integration and want it complete, but no bigger than what was asked"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "writes down what must work and what's out of scope, reuses the existing code, and leaves out options, config and polish nobody asked for"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "a narrow feature working end to end, tested against what was asked, plus a note of any omission that matters"
            },
            {
              "label": "Unlike surgical-patch",
              "text": "which corrects existing behavior in one place, this adds new behavior through all the code involved"
            },
            {
              "label": "Runs",
              "text": "on its own, whenever the agent sees a task that fits; it's a short list of rules, not a step-by-step guide"
            }
          ],
          "text": "The agent derives acceptance criteria and explicit non-goals from your request and the repository. It follows the feature from where it starts through every part of the code involved and builds one complete path, rather than cramming it into one file or a local patch. It hooks into existing code where that fits, and refactors only when patching would duplicate behavior or blur which code is responsible for what. Extra modes, options, configuration and polish stay out unless the criteria need them, and any new dependency, service or migration needs a stated reason. It runs the new path, runs a focused test, and stops as soon as the criteria pass.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/juliusbrussee/caveman/tree/bad48a0e1df55e05e3f52249afa35ecc1909ee76/skills/lean-build"
        },
        "tags": [
          "category:engineering",
          "type:workflow",
          "effect:local",
          "needs:none",
          "by:community",
          "implementation",
          "scope-control"
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "juliusbrussee/caveman/skills/migration",
      "name": "migration",
      "skills.sh": {
        "url": "https://skills.sh/juliusbrussee/caveman/migration",
        "installs": 115923,
        "weeklyInstalls": [12335, 12946, 10759, 9982, 8186, 7065, 7503, 7111],
        "firstSeen": "2026-08-11"
      },
      "skillMd": {
        "version": "bad48a0e1df55e05e3f52249afa35ecc1909ee76",
        "updated": "2026-10-20",
        "skew": 0
      },
      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Implements reversible migrations of schemas, data, APIs and dependencies.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "you're changing a database schema, data format, API, protocol, config or dependency that other code relies on"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "maps who reads and writes the current form, then plans both a forward path and a rollback before editing"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "a migration done in stages, with old and new versions able to run side by side and each stage verified"
            },
            {
              "label": "Won't",
              "text": "delete old data or remove the old path unless you separately ask for that step"
            },
            {
              "label": "Runs",
              "text": "on its own, whenever the agent sees a task that fits; it's a short list of rules, not a step-by-step guide"
            }
          ],
          "text": "Before editing, the agent maps the current readers, writers and data shape, how long old and new versions must coexist, and who owns each part. It defines a forward path and a rollback path, keeps existing data intact, and makes any destructive step explicit and separately authorized. If old and new versions may run at the same time during a rollout, both must keep working. It follows the expand, migrate, verify, contract order: add the new form next to the old one, move over, check, and only then remove the old. Retries must be idempotent, and partial failures visible. It verifies the old and new paths at each stage and stops at the stage you asked for: if you asked only to add the new form, removing the old one is a separate request.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/juliusbrussee/caveman/tree/bad48a0e1df55e05e3f52249afa35ecc1909ee76/skills/migration"
        },
        "tags": [
          "category:engineering",
          "type:workflow",
          "effect:local",
          "needs:none",
          "by:community",
          "migration"
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "mattpocock/skills/skills/engineering/triage",
      "name": "triage",
      "skills.sh": {
        "url": "https://skills.sh/mattpocock/skills/triage",
        "installs": 1047433,
        "weeklyInstalls": [38590, 35937, 33093, 36289, 46500, 37352, 33488, 30497],
        "firstSeen": "2026-04-28"
      },
      "skillMd": {
        "version": "5beb35b040e3de97e54fa9e386d7e42d15c295c2",
        "updated": "2026-10-20",
        "skew": 0
      },
      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Moves issues and external PRs through a triage state machine and writes agent-ready briefs.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "issues and outside pull requests pile up on your tracker and you want help sorting them"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "gives each item two labels, a category (bug or enhancement) and a state such as needs-info, ready-for-agent or wontfix, and moves it along"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "labels, comments and closed issues on your tracker, plus briefs specific enough for an agent to build from without you"
            },
            {
              "label": "Checks",
              "text": "whether a request is already built or was rejected before, and reproduces a bug before going further"
            },
            {
              "label": "Needs",
              "text": "an issue tracker and labels configured with setup-matt-pocock-skills, the author's one-time repo setup"
            },
            {
              "label": "Runs",
              "text": "only when you invoke it; it recommends, then waits for your decision"
            }
          ],
          "text": "You call /triage and say what you want in plain words: what needs my attention, look at #42, move #42 to ready-for-agent. For an overview it lists untriaged items, items under evaluation, and items whose reporter replied since the last triage notes. For one issue or outside pull request, it reads everything, searches the code for an existing implementation and the repo's .out-of-scope/ folder, where past rejections are written up, then recommends a category and state and waits for you. Before going further it checks the claim: it reproduces the bug, or runs the pull request's code. If the request is vague, it questions you round by round until it's clear. Then it acts on the state: ready-for-agent gets an agent brief, a comment with current and desired behavior, the interfaces to change and testable acceptance criteria, free of file paths so it stays valid as code moves; needs-info gets notes on what's settled and specific questions for the reporter; a rejected feature is written up in .out-of-scope/ so it isn't argued again. Every comment it posts opens with a note that AI wrote it.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/mattpocock/skills/tree/5beb35b040e3de97e54fa9e386d7e42d15c295c2/skills/engineering/triage"
        },
        "tags": [
          "category:debugging",
          "type:workflow",
          "effect:remote",
          "needs:issue-tracker",
          "by:community",
          "issue-tracking",
          "code-review"
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "mattpocock/skills/skills/engineering/diagnosing-bugs",
      "name": "diagnosing-bugs",
      "skills.sh": {
        "url": "https://skills.sh/mattpocock/skills/diagnosing-bugs",
        "installs": 900453,
        "weeklyInstalls": [42055, 40276, 35844, 41316, 53687, 42643, 37477, 34921],
        "firstSeen": "2026-06-04"
      },
      "skillMd": {
        "version": "5beb35b040e3de97e54fa9e386d7e42d15c295c2",
        "updated": "2026-10-20",
        "skew": 0
      },
      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Runs a diagnosis loop for hard bugs and performance regressions.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "a bug is hard to pin down, or something got slower, and guessing at fixes isn't working"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "first builds one fast command that fails on this exact bug, and forms no theory until that command exists"
            },
            {
              "label": "Phases",
              "text": "build that command, shrink the repro, rank guesses, test one variable at a time, fix with a test, clean up"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "a fix with a regression test, debug logs removed, and the confirmed cause written in the commit message"
            }
          ],
          "text": "Its premise is that a hard bug gets easy once you have a feedback loop: one command that fails on this exact bug and passes once it's fixed. So the agent puts most of its effort there, trying a failing test, a curl script, a headless browser, a replayed request, a fuzz loop or a git bisect script, then making it faster and deterministic. When only a person can trigger the bug, a bundled script walks you through the clicks and records what you saw. If no loop can be built, it stops and asks you for access or a captured log. Next it shrinks the repro to the smallest case that still fails, shows you three to five ranked guesses at the cause, each with a prediction that could prove it wrong, and tests them one variable at a time, using debug logs with a unique tag so they're easy to remove, or measurements for slowdowns. The regression test comes before the fix, but only where a test can reproduce the real pattern; if none can, that's reported as a finding. Secrets are redacted from anything it shows.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/mattpocock/skills/tree/5beb35b040e3de97e54fa9e386d7e42d15c295c2/skills/engineering/diagnosing-bugs"
        },
        "tags": [
          "category:debugging",
          "type:workflow",
          "effect:local",
          "needs:none",
          "by:community",
          "debugging",
          "performance"
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "mattpocock/skills/skills/engineering/code-review",
      "name": "code-review",
      "skills.sh": {
        "url": "https://skills.sh/mattpocock/skills/code-review",
        "installs": 860159,
        "weeklyInstalls": [45428, 42525, 38032, 39868, 53340, 44784, 38605, 38125],
        "firstSeen": "2026-07-01"
      },
      "skillMd": {
        "version": "a496f5513d4a4cf889bc48e189e579f4b0bdf6aa",
        "updated": "2026-10-30",
        "skew": 8
      },
      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Reviews changes since a fixed point against the repo's standards and the spec.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "you want a branch, PR or work in progress reviewed: everything since a commit, branch or tag you name"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "runs two reviews in parallel subagents: one against the repo's coding standards, one against the issue or spec"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "two separate reports, Standards and Spec, and a line naming the worst issue in each"
            },
            {
              "label": "Needs",
              "text": "subagents; for specs kept in issues, the issue-tracker setup that comes with this skill set"
            }
          ],
          "text": "It reviews the diff between the point you name and HEAD. First it finds the spec: an issue referenced in the commits, a path you give, or a matching file under docs/ or specs/; if there is none, the spec review is skipped. It gathers every file that documents how code should be written, such as CODING_STANDARDS.md or CONTRIBUTING.md, and adds twelve code smells from Martin Fowler's book Refactoring, such as a method that uses another object's data more than its own, or abstractions added for needs nobody has. The repo's own rules override these. Two subagents then work in parallel. The Standards one cites each broken rule and flags smells as judgement calls, not violations. The Spec one lists what's missing, what was added without being asked, and what looks implemented wrongly, quoting the spec. The two reports are kept apart and never re-ranked, because code can follow every rule and still build the wrong thing, or the reverse. It only reports; it doesn't change the code.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/mattpocock/skills/tree/5beb35b040e3de97e54fa9e386d7e42d15c295c2/skills/engineering/code-review"
        },
        "tags": [
          "category:debugging",
          "type:workflow",
          "effect:none",
          "needs:subagents",
          "needs:issue-tracker",
          "by:community",
          "code-review"
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "juliusbrussee/caveman/skills/caveman-review",
      "name": "caveman-review",
      "skills.sh": {
        "url": "https://skills.sh/juliusbrussee/caveman/caveman-review",
        "installs": 424891,
        "weeklyInstalls": [13301, 14058, 11810, 10875, 9223, 7869, 8232, 7790],
        "firstSeen": "2026-04-08"
      },
      "skillMd": {
        "version": "bad48a0e1df55e05e3f52249afa35ecc1909ee76",
        "updated": "2026-10-20",
        "skew": 0
      },
      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Writes terse code review comments, one line per finding.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "you want code review comments short enough to paste straight into a pull request"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "writes one line per finding: the line number, the problem and a concrete fix, optionally tagged as a bug, risk, nit or question"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "comments ready to paste, like \"L42: 🔴 bug: user can be null after .find(). Add guard before .email.\""
            },
            {
              "label": "Switches to prose",
              "text": "for security findings, design disagreements and authors new to the code who need the why"
            },
            {
              "label": "Won't",
              "text": "write the fix, approve the pull request or run linters"
            }
          ],
          "text": "Caveman is a skill set whose main mode makes the agent reply in short, plain fragments to save tokens. That mode keeps anything saved outside the chat, PR comments included, in normal prose; this skill makes review comments terse too. Each finding is one line, L<line>: <problem>. <fix>., with the file name added on diffs that span several files, and an optional severity: 🔴 bug for broken behavior, 🟡 risk for fragile code such as a race or a swallowed error, 🔵 nit for style the author can ignore, ❓ q for a genuine question. It drops openers like \"I noticed that\", hedging, praise on every comment and descriptions of what the line already does. It keeps exact line numbers, function and variable names in backticks, and a concrete fix rather than \"consider refactoring\". Security issues, architectural disagreements and reviews for newcomers get a normal paragraph, then the terse lines resume. It stays in this style until you say \"stop caveman-review\" or \"normal mode\".",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/juliusbrussee/caveman/tree/bad48a0e1df55e05e3f52249afa35ecc1909ee76/skills/caveman-review"
        },
        "tags": [
          "category:debugging",
          "category:output-style",
          "type:guidelines",
          "effect:none",
          "needs:none",
          "by:community",
          "code-review",
          "output-style",
          "token-saving"
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "obra/superpowers/skills/systematic-debugging",
      "name": "systematic-debugging",
      "skills.sh": {
        "url": "https://skills.sh/obra/superpowers/systematic-debugging",
        "installs": 322651,
        "weeklyInstalls": [7573, 7805, 7836, 8538, 8901, 11211, 9353, 8558],
        "firstSeen": "2026-01-19"
      },
      "skillMd": {
        "version": "bb92a77741419a4ab5f06e711a283343f1ada0c3",
        "updated": "2026-10-20",
        "skew": 0
      },
      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Finds the root cause before proposing any fix.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "a bug, failing test or odd behavior shows up, especially when a quick fix is tempting"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "forbids any fix until the root cause is found, through four phases in order"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "a fix for the cause, backed by a failing test that now passes"
            },
            {
              "label": "Stops",
              "text": "guess-and-retry loops: after three failed fixes, it questions the design with you"
            }
          ],
          "text": "Its rule is \"no fixes without root cause investigation first\". The agent works through four phases in order. First it investigates: it reads the error in full, reproduces it, checks recent changes and, in a system with several parts, logs what enters and leaves each one to find where it breaks. Then it compares the broken code with similar code that works, forms one hypothesis and tests it with the smallest possible change. Only then does it fix: it writes a failing test, makes one change and verifies it. If three fixes have failed, it stops and discusses the architecture with you instead of trying a fourth.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/obra/superpowers/tree/bb92a77741419a4ab5f06e711a283343f1ada0c3/skills/systematic-debugging"
        },
        "tags": [
          "category:debugging",
          "type:workflow",
          "effect:local",
          "needs:none",
          "by:community",
          "debugging",
          "agent-workflows"
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "obra/superpowers/skills/requesting-code-review",
      "name": "requesting-code-review",
      "skills.sh": {
        "url": "https://skills.sh/obra/superpowers/requesting-code-review",
        "installs": 273734,
        "weeklyInstalls": [5980, 6304, 6243, 5700, 5968, 8836, 6442, 5994],
        "firstSeen": "2026-01-19"
      },
      "skillMd": {
        "version": "bb92a77741419a4ab5f06e711a283343f1ada0c3",
        "updated": "2026-10-20",
        "skew": 0
      },
      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Sends finished work to a reviewer subagent before merging.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "a task or feature is done, before merging, or when you're stuck and want fresh eyes"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "sends a reviewer subagent the commit range and the plan, with none of the session's history"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "strengths, issues graded Critical, Important or Minor with file and line, and a verdict on whether it's ready to merge"
            },
            {
              "label": "Then",
              "text": "the agent fixes Critical issues at once and Important ones before going on, notes Minor ones, and argues back with code or tests when the reviewer is wrong"
            },
            {
              "label": "Unlike receiving-code-review",
              "text": "which covers how to act on feedback you've been given; this one asks for the review"
            }
          ],
          "text": "The agent fills the reviewer prompt that ships with the skill with what was built, what it was meant to do, and the first and last commits of the work, then starts a subagent with it. The reviewer reads the diff without changing the checkout or starting reviewers of its own. It checks that the code matches the plan, then its quality, architecture, tests and readiness for production. Where the spec says nothing, it judges by what a reasonable user would expect. It also names anything it decided was out of scope, so the agent can rule on it rather than lose it. subagent-driven-development and executing-plans use the same reviewer prompt for their final review of the whole branch.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/obra/superpowers/tree/bb92a77741419a4ab5f06e711a283343f1ada0c3/skills/requesting-code-review"
        },
        "tags": [
          "category:debugging",
          "type:workflow",
          "effect:none",
          "needs:subagents",
          "by:community",
          "code-review",
          "multi-agent",
          "agent-workflows"
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "obra/superpowers/skills/receiving-code-review",
      "name": "receiving-code-review",
      "skills.sh": {
        "url": "https://skills.sh/obra/superpowers/receiving-code-review",
        "installs": 232351,
        "weeklyInstalls": [5661, 5708, 5291, 5227, 4871, 6391, 5894, 5544],
        "firstSeen": "2026-01-19"
      },
      "skillMd": {
        "version": "bb92a77741419a4ab5f06e711a283343f1ada0c3",
        "updated": "2026-10-20",
        "skew": 0
      },
      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Evaluates review feedback technically before implementing it.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "you get review feedback, from a person or a reviewer agent, especially if it seems unclear or wrong"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "makes the agent check each suggestion against the code before changing anything, and push back with reasons when it's wrong"
            },
            {
              "label": "Forbids",
              "text": "\"You're absolutely right!\", \"Great point!\" and any thanks: it states the fix, or just makes it"
            },
            {
              "label": "Asks first",
              "text": "about every unclear item before implementing any of them, since items may be related"
            }
          ],
          "text": "Its stance is technical correctness over social comfort. The agent reads all the feedback, restates each point in its own words or asks about it, checks it against the code, and decides whether it's right for this codebase. Feedback from you, the user, is trusted once understood. An outside reviewer's is checked: does it break something, is there a reason for the current code, does the reviewer have the full context? When a reviewer asks for a fuller implementation of something, it checks whether anything uses it and suggests removing it if nothing does. Once every item is clear, fixes go in one at a time, blocking issues first, each tested. If a suggestion conflicts with decisions you made, it stops and asks you. When its pushback turns out wrong, it says so in a sentence and moves on. On GitHub, it answers inline comments in their own thread.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/obra/superpowers/tree/bb92a77741419a4ab5f06e711a283343f1ada0c3/skills/receiving-code-review"
        },
        "tags": [
          "category:debugging",
          "type:guidelines",
          "effect:none",
          "needs:none",
          "by:community",
          "code-review"
        ]
      }
    },
    {
