    {
      "id": "mattpocock/skills/skills/engineering/retro",
      "name": "retro",
      "skills.sh": {
        "url": "https://skills.sh/mattpocock/skills/retro",
        "installs": 332142,
        "weeklyInstalls": [27970, 26950, 23862, 29987, 44589, 36804, 32260, 29914],
        "firstSeen": "2026-08-24"
      },
      "skillMd": {
        "version": "5beb35b040e3de97e54fa9e386d7e42d15c295c2",
        "updated": "2026-10-20",
        "skew": 0
      },
      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Runs a retrospective on a coding session and improves the agent's setup.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "a coding session went badly or slowly and you want the next one to go better"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "reads that session's logs and looks for fixes to the agent's setup, not to the code it wrote"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "a list of suggested changes, most severe first, such as a lint check to add or a rule to move out of AGENTS.md"
            },
            {
              "label": "Needs",
              "text": "writing-for-agents, a sibling skill on writing docs for agents"
            },
            {
              "label": "Runs",
              "text": "only when you call it"
            }
          ],
          "text": "A retrospective on how the agent worked, aimed at its environment: the repo's files, checks and tools. It reads the session you name, from session logs on your machine, or the current one by default, and looks for seven kinds of improvement: files that were hard to find, mistakes an automated check could have caught, mistakes the review agent should have caught, an oversized AGENTS.md, expensive tool calls, instructions that don't change the agent's behavior, and information the agent couldn't reach, such as dev server logs. It favors deterministic checks: a mechanical mistake gets a lint rule, a pre-commit hook or a CI job rather than a written rule, and a repo with no lint, type check or tests run by a hook or CI is flagged on its own. Judgement calls go in a CODING_STANDARDS.md that the reviewer reads, and AGENTS.md is kept to short pointers to other files. It stops at suggestions; making the changes is up to you.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/mattpocock/skills/tree/5beb35b040e3de97e54fa9e386d7e42d15c295c2/skills/engineering/retro"
        },
        "tags": [
          "category:debugging",
          "type:workflow",
          "effect:none",
          "needs:none",
          "by:community",
          "self-improvement",
          "skill-authoring"
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "juliusbrussee/caveman/skills/investigate-first",
      "name": "investigate-first",
      "skills.sh": {
        "url": "https://skills.sh/juliusbrussee/caveman/investigate-first",
        "installs": 117633,
        "weeklyInstalls": [12507, 13144, 10916, 10142, 8346, 7199, 7582, 7204],
        "firstSeen": "2026-08-11"
      },
      "skillMd": {
        "version": "bad48a0e1df55e05e3f52249afa35ecc1909ee76",
        "updated": "2026-10-20",
        "skew": 0
      },
      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Diagnoses ambiguous failures before editing.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "something fails and the cause is unclear: an intermittent bug, an odd behavior or a performance regression"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "separates what was observed from what's guessed, and weighs each explanation by its evidence and by how cheap it is to test"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "the cause and the evidence for it, or the exact blocker, with no code changed unless the task asks for a fix"
            },
            {
              "label": "Unlike surgical-patch",
              "text": "which goes on to fix the bug, this stops at the diagnosis"
            },
            {
              "label": "Runs",
              "text": "on its own, whenever the agent sees a task that fits; it's a short list of rules, not a step-by-step guide"
            }
          ],
          "text": "The agent touches no product code until the problem is understood. It keeps the observed symptom apart from the cause it suspects, and traces inputs, state changes, which part of the code is responsible for what, and the failure output. It ranks its hypotheses by the evidence for them and by how cheaply each could be ruled out, and won't edit anything until one explanation accounts for all the evidence. It stops exploring once it can name the cause or the exact thing blocking it, and reports the cause with its proof.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/juliusbrussee/caveman/tree/bad48a0e1df55e05e3f52249afa35ecc1909ee76/skills/investigate-first"
        },
        "tags": [
          "category:debugging",
          "type:workflow",
          "effect:none",
          "needs:none",
          "by:community",
          "debugging"
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "juliusbrussee/caveman/skills/caveman",
      "name": "caveman",
      "skills.sh": {
        "url": "https://skills.sh/juliusbrussee/caveman/caveman",
        "installs": 613807,
        "weeklyInstalls": [18867, 19729, 16919, 15394, 14192, 12197, 12419, 11908],
        "firstSeen": "2026-04-04"
      },
      "skillMd": {
        "version": "bad48a0e1df55e05e3f52249afa35ecc1909ee76",
        "updated": "2026-10-20",
        "skew": 0
      },
      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Switches to ultra-compressed answers that keep technical accuracy.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "replies feel padded, or you pay per token"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "makes the agent answer first, in short plain sentences, with no greetings, hedging or recaps"
            },
            {
              "label": "Keeps",
              "text": "code, commands, numbers, errors and every \"not\" exact"
            },
            {
              "label": "Runs",
              "text": "for the whole session, until you say \"stop caveman\""
            }
          ],
          "text": "Despite the name, it's a writing discipline more than a joke voice: each reply leads with the answer, then the reason, then the next step. It cuts ceremony (\"Sure!\", \"Let me…\", \"Hope this helps\"), filler words and invented abbreviations, and may drop articles where a sentence still reads in one pass, but never a negation or a number. It only changes chat replies: code, commits and docs are written normally, and it switches back to full sentences for security warnings, irreversible actions and questions to you. Once on, it stays on until you turn it off.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/juliusbrussee/caveman/tree/bad48a0e1df55e05e3f52249afa35ecc1909ee76/skills/caveman"
        },
        "tags": [
          "category:output-style",
          "type:mode",
          "effect:none",
          "needs:none",
          "by:community",
          "token-saving",
          "output-style"
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "juliusbrussee/caveman/skills/caveman-commit",
      "name": "caveman-commit",
      "skills.sh": {
        "url": "https://skills.sh/juliusbrussee/caveman/caveman-commit",
        "installs": 428912,
        "weeklyInstalls": [13593, 14217, 12027, 10978, 9251, 7911, 8218, 7804],
        "firstSeen": "2026-04-08"
      },
      "skillMd": {
        "version": "bad48a0e1df55e05e3f52249afa35ecc1909ee76",
        "updated": "2026-10-20",
        "skew": 0
      },
      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Writes Conventional Commits messages compressed to intent only.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "you want a short commit message that skips what the diff already shows"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "writes a Conventional Commits message, like \"feat(api): add GET /users/:id/profile\", with a subject of 50 characters when possible"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "the message in a code block to paste; it never stages, commits or amends"
            },
            {
              "label": "Adds a body",
              "text": "only to explain why, and always for breaking changes, security fixes, data migrations and reverts"
            },
            {
              "label": "Leaves out",
              "text": "\"I\", \"we\", \"this commit\", emoji and AI attribution, unless your project's rules ask for them"
            }
          ],
          "text": "Caveman is a skill set whose main mode makes the agent reply in short, plain fragments to save tokens. That mode keeps commit messages in normal prose; this skill makes them terse too. The subject follows Conventional Commits, with a type such as feat, fix, refactor or docs, in the imperative (\"add\", not \"added\"), at most 50 characters when possible and never over 72, with no trailing period. A body is added only for a reason the diff doesn't show, a breaking change, migration notes or linked issues, wrapped at 72 characters and ending with references like \"Closes #42\". It drops whatever the diff already says, such as \"this commit does X\" or a file name the scope already gives. Breaking changes, security fixes, data migrations and reverts always get a body, for whoever debugs them later. It only writes the message; you run git. Say \"stop caveman-commit\" to get verbose messages back.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/juliusbrussee/caveman/tree/bad48a0e1df55e05e3f52249afa35ecc1909ee76/skills/caveman-commit"
        },
        "tags": [
          "category:output-style",
          "category:engineering",
          "type:command",
          "effect:none",
          "needs:none",
          "by:community",
          "stack:git",
          "git",
          "output-style",
          "token-saving"
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "juliusbrussee/caveman/skills/caveman-compress",
      "name": "caveman-compress",
      "skills.sh": {
        "url": "https://skills.sh/juliusbrussee/caveman/caveman-compress",
        "installs": 425971,
        "weeklyInstalls": [13406, 14243, 11973, 11023, 9702, 8502, 8659, 8294],
        "firstSeen": "2026-04-06"
      },
      "skillMd": {
        "version": "bad48a0e1df55e05e3f52249afa35ecc1909ee76",
        "updated": "2026-10-20",
        "skew": 0
      },
      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Compresses CLAUDE.md or todo files into caveman format to save input tokens.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "a file the agent reads every session, such as CLAUDE.md or a todo list, costs too many input tokens"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "rewrites the file's prose as terse fragments, dropping articles and filler, while code, commands, paths, URLs and headings stay exactly as they were"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "the compressed file in place of the original, with a readable backup kept outside the project"
            },
            {
              "label": "Sends",
              "text": "the whole file to a model provider: the claude CLI, the Anthropic API or an OpenAI-compatible endpoint; names that look like secrets are refused"
            },
            {
              "label": "Needs",
              "text": "Python, and a file path from you; it won't compress pasted text"
            }
          ],
          "text": "Caveman is a skill set whose main mode makes the agent reply in short, plain fragments to save tokens. This skill applies the same style to files the agent reads, rather than the replies it writes. You run /caveman-compress with a file path; a bundled Python script sends the file to your configured model, checks the output, has the model fix only what failed, up to twice, and leaves the original untouched if it still fails. Compression drops articles, filler, pleasantries, hedging and connecting words, uses short synonyms and fragments, and merges bullets that repeat each other. Code blocks, inline code, URLs, paths, commands, names, numbers and the markdown structure stay exact. It only compresses prose files such as .md or .txt, never code or config, and only the prose parts of mixed files. The original is saved as FILE.original.md in a caveman-compress folder in your user data directory, not next to the file, so the agent doesn't load it again.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/juliusbrussee/caveman/tree/bad48a0e1df55e05e3f52249afa35ecc1909ee76/skills/caveman-compress"
        },
        "tags": [
          "category:output-style",
          "type:command",
          "effect:local",
          "needs:account",
          "by:community",
          "token-saving"
        ],
        "note": "Sends the file's full text to a model provider to compress it."
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "juliusbrussee/caveman/skills/caveman-help",
      "name": "caveman-help",
      "skills.sh": {
        "url": "https://skills.sh/juliusbrussee/caveman/caveman-help",
        "installs": 415630,
        "weeklyInstalls": [12986, 13998, 11535, 10903, 9587, 7928, 8126, 7652],
        "firstSeen": "2026-04-11"
      },
      "skillMd": {
        "version": "bad48a0e1df55e05e3f52249afa35ecc1909ee76",
        "updated": "2026-10-20",
        "skew": 0
      },
      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Shows a quick-reference card for caveman modes.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "you've installed Caveman, a skill set that makes agents reply tersely to save tokens, and want a reminder of its modes and commands"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "prints one reference card, itself in caveman style, without changing the mode or saving anything"
            },
            {
              "label": "Lists",
              "text": "the three modes, the commit, review and compress commands, how to stop caveman, and how to change the default mode"
            },
            {
              "label": "Runs",
              "text": "when you type /caveman-help or ask for caveman help"
            }
          ],
          "text": "A one-shot cheat sheet for the Caveman skills. It shows the three modes: caveman, which answers first and drops fluff; ultracave, which strips grammar down to fragments; and megacave, which replies in classical Chinese, using far fewer characters, with technical terms kept as they are. It lists /caveman-commit, /caveman-review and /caveman-compress, reminds you that \"stop caveman\" or \"normal mode\" turns it off, and notes that replies stay in your language. It also explains how to set the default mode with the CAVEMAN_DEFAULT_MODE variable or ~/.config/caveman/config.json, including \"manual\", which starts sessions with caveman off until you type /caveman. It's only useful once the other Caveman skills are installed.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/juliusbrussee/caveman/tree/bad48a0e1df55e05e3f52249afa35ecc1909ee76/skills/caveman-help"
        },
        "tags": [
          "category:output-style",
          "type:command",
          "effect:none",
          "needs:none",
          "by:community",
          "output-style"
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "juliusbrussee/caveman/skills/cavecrew",
      "name": "cavecrew",
      "skills.sh": {
        "url": "https://skills.sh/juliusbrussee/caveman/cavecrew",
        "installs": 371607,
        "weeklyInstalls": [12812, 13425, 11186, 10519, 9349, 7810, 7980, 7538],
        "firstSeen": "2026-05-01"
      },
      "skillMd": {
        "version": "bad48a0e1df55e05e3f52249afa35ecc1909ee76",
        "updated": "2026-10-20",
        "skew": 0
      },
      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Delegates work to terse investigator, builder and reviewer subagents.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "the agent hands many searches, small edits or reviews to subagents and its main context keeps filling up"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "tells the agent when to use three terse subagents, a code finder, a one-or-two-file editor and a diff reviewer, instead of working inline"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "subagent reports in short fixed formats, such as file:line lists, so each handoff costs the main context far fewer tokens"
            },
            {
              "label": "Needs",
              "text": "the Caveman plugin installed: the three subagents come with it, not with this skill"
            },
            {
              "label": "Unlike default subagents",
              "text": "such as Claude Code's built-in Explore search agent, which reply in prose; those stay the choice when you want suggestions or architecture advice"
            }
          ],
          "text": "Caveman is a skill set and plugin that makes agents terse to save tokens. Subagent results land in the main context word for word, so a search that returns 2,000 tokens of prose costs 2,000 tokens every time. cavecrew is a guide to three Caveman subagents that answer in clipped fragments. cavecrew-investigator finds where something is defined or used and returns a file:line list. cavecrew-builder makes a small edit in one or two files whose location is already known, and reports what changed and whether a re-read confirmed it, or turns the task down as too big. cavecrew-reviewer reviews a diff and returns one line per finding, with a tally by severity. The usual chain is locate, fix, review, and broad searches can run two or three investigators at once. New features, edits across three or more files, and reviews that need rationale stay in the main conversation or go to the default agents. The subagents switch to plain English for security warnings and irreversible actions.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/juliusbrussee/caveman/tree/bad48a0e1df55e05e3f52249afa35ecc1909ee76/skills/cavecrew"
        },
        "tags": [
