    {
      "id": "mattpocock/skills/skills/in-progress/writing-shape",
      "name": "writing-shape",
      "skills.sh": {
        "url": "https://skills.sh/mattpocock/skills/writing-shape",
        "installs": 522797,
        "weeklyInstalls": [28154, 26445, 23652, 23334, 29018, 25556, 23124, 21326],
        "firstSeen": "2026-05-06"
      },
      "skillMd": {
        "version": "5beb35b040e3de97e54fa9e386d7e42d15c295c2",
        "updated": "2026-10-20",
        "skew": 0
      },
      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Shapes raw material into an article, paragraph by paragraph.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "you have a pile of notes and want a first draft"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "settles an opening with you, then builds the article one paragraph at a time"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "an article in its own file; your notes file is never edited"
            },
            {
              "label": "Unlike writing-beats",
              "text": "you commit to an angle up front, then debate each block's form: prose, list, table or quote"
            },
            {
              "label": "Runs",
              "text": "only when you call it"
            }
          ],
          "text": "It reads your whole notes file first, whatever form it takes: a list of fragments, a wall of prose, a transcript. After agreeing with you on what readers already know, it drafts two or three openings that each imply a different angle, and makes you pick one or a mix. From then on it keeps asking what the reader needs to hear next, pulls the answer from your notes, and argues with you whether that block should be a paragraph, a list, a table, a callout or a quote. Each agreed block is appended right away, so you watch the article grow, and like writing-beats it never lets a block rely on an idea that hasn't been explained yet.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/mattpocock/skills/tree/5beb35b040e3de97e54fa9e386d7e42d15c295c2/skills/in-progress/writing-shape"
        },
        "tags": [
          "category:writing",
          "type:workflow",
          "effect:local",
          "needs:none",
          "by:community",
          "writing"
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "mattpocock/skills/skills/in-progress/writing-fragments",
      "name": "writing-fragments",
      "skills.sh": {
        "url": "https://skills.sh/mattpocock/skills/writing-fragments",
        "installs": 522041,
        "weeklyInstalls": [28171, 26398, 23622, 23194, 28887, 25480, 23063, 21227],
        "firstSeen": "2026-05-06"
      },
      "skillMd": {
        "version": "5beb35b040e3de97e54fa9e386d7e42d15c295c2",
        "updated": "2026-10-20",
        "skew": 0
      },
      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Interviews you to mine raw fragments before any structure exists.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "you want to write an essay or article but don't know its shape yet"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "interviews you and saves every usable line, anecdote and idea to one Markdown file"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "a file of raw notes, with no outline"
            },
            {
              "label": "Runs",
              "text": "only when you call it"
            }
          ],
          "text": "This is the exploring half of Matt Pocock's writing workflow. You say what you'd like to write about, and the agent keeps asking: what you noticed, what happened, what you meant by a phrase you used. Anything that might survive into the final piece goes into one Markdown file: a sharp sentence, a claim with its reason, an anecdote, a quote, a half-formed thought. It pushes hardest for one coined phrase that names your idea, since the whole piece can later hang on it. It appends without asking, rereads the file before every write so your own edits survive, and never imposes headings, an outline or an order. Structure is left to writing-beats or writing-shape.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/mattpocock/skills/tree/5beb35b040e3de97e54fa9e386d7e42d15c295c2/skills/in-progress/writing-fragments"
        },
        "tags": [
          "category:writing",
          "type:workflow",
          "effect:local",
          "needs:none",
          "by:community",
          "writing",
          "interview"
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "anthropics/skills/skills/doc-coauthoring",
      "name": "doc-coauthoring",
      "skills.sh": {
        "url": "https://skills.sh/anthropics/skills/doc-coauthoring",
        "installs": 98639,
        "weeklyInstalls": [1554, 1594, 1470, 1722, 1948, 2104, 2412, 2416],
        "firstSeen": "2026-01-20"
      },
      "skillMd": {
        "version": "dbd4588f9e1033efb41dad4bef2f7947c8993d44",
        "updated": "2026-10-20",
        "skew": 0
      },
      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Walks you through co-authoring docs, proposals and specs.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "you need to write a spec, proposal, design doc, RFC or decision doc that other people will read"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "guides you through three stages: dumping context, building each section from brainstormed options, then testing the doc on a fresh Claude"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "a finished doc, in a Markdown file or a claude.ai artifact (a document shown beside the chat), checked by a reader without your context"
            },
            {
              "label": "Reader testing",
              "text": "a Claude that sees only the doc answers the questions real readers would ask, exposing gaps and ambiguities"
            },
            {
              "label": "Asks first",
              "text": "whether you want the structured workflow; if not, it just writes with you freeform"
            }
          ],
          "text": "A structured workflow from Anthropic for documents others will read. It offers itself when you start one, and you can decline. First you say what the doc is, who reads it and what it should achieve, then dump everything you know in any order: background, past discussions, rejected alternatives, politics. Connected tools such as Slack or Google Drive can be read directly. The agent asks 5 to 10 numbered questions until it understands the trade-offs. Next it creates the doc with placeholder sections and fills them one at a time, starting with the most uncertain: it asks questions, brainstorms 5 to 20 things the section could include, lets you keep, cut or merge them, drafts, then refines with targeted edits rather than rewrites. You're asked to say what to change rather than edit yourself, so it learns your style. Last comes reader testing: it predicts what readers would ask and puts those questions to a Claude that has only the doc and none of the conversation, through a subagent if it can or by you in a new chat if not, then fixes whatever that reader got wrong.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/anthropics/skills/tree/dbd4588f9e1033efb41dad4bef2f7947c8993d44/skills/doc-coauthoring"
        },
        "tags": [
          "category:writing",
          "type:workflow",
          "effect:local",
          "needs:none",
          "by:official",
          "docs-writing",
          "writing"
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "anthropics/skills/skills/internal-comms",
      "name": "internal-comms",
      "skills.sh": {
        "url": "https://skills.sh/anthropics/skills/internal-comms",
        "installs": 84036,
        "weeklyInstalls": [1201, 1312, 1249, 1328, 1412, 1623, 1946, 2225],
        "firstSeen": "2026-01-19"
      },
      "skillMd": {
        "version": "dbd4588f9e1033efb41dad4bef2f7947c8993d44",
        "updated": "2026-10-20",
        "skew": 0
      },
      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Writes internal communications in your company's formats.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "you need a team status update, a company newsletter, an FAQ or another internal message"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "picks the matching format from its example files and gathers facts from Slack, email, Drive or calendar when connected"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "a draft in a fixed format, such as a 3P update: Progress, Plans and Problems, one to three sentences each"
            },
            {
              "label": "Formats",
              "text": "3P team updates, company-wide newsletters and FAQ roundups, plus general advice for anything else"
            }
          ],
          "text": "The SKILL.md only works out what kind of message you want and loads one of four guides. A 3P update covers a team's week in three short lines, Progress, Plans and Problems, with metrics where possible, so leadership can read it in under a minute. A newsletter is about 20 to 25 short bullets for the whole company, written as \"we\", grouped into sections and full of links to the docs, announcements and threads that drew the most attention. An FAQ collects the questions many employees are asking and answers each in a sentence or two, flagging any that need an executive. Anything else, from incident reports to leadership updates, gets general advice: ask about audience, purpose and tone, and lead with what matters most. Where it can, the agent pulls material from Slack, email, Google Drive and calendar, favouring posts with many reactions and widely attended meetings; otherwise it asks you. The formats are Anthropic's, written down in four Markdown files in the skill; to match how your company writes, you edit those files.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/anthropics/skills/tree/dbd4588f9e1033efb41dad4bef2f7947c8993d44/skills/internal-comms"
        },
        "tags": [
          "category:writing",
          "type:guidelines",
          "effect:none",
          "needs:none",
          "by:official",
          "writing"
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "vercel-labs/agent-skills/skills/writing-guidelines",
      "name": "writing-guidelines",
      "skills.sh": {
        "url": "https://skills.sh/vercel-labs/agent-skills/writing-guidelines",
        "installs": 119804,
        "weeklyInstalls": [4367, 6263, 6155, 7184, 6611, 7037, 8760, 7940],
        "firstSeen": "2026-06-01"
      },
      "skillMd": {
        "version": "063bee94c3f4df8453406c830b0a7df0f2860278",
        "updated": "2026-10-20",
        "skew": 0
      },
      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Reviews docs and prose against Vercel's writing guidelines.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "you want docs or other prose checked against Vercel's writing handbook"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "fetches the latest rules from Vercel's writing-guidelines repo, then reviews the files you name against every one"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "terse findings grouped by file, each at a clickable file:line"
            },
            {
              "label": "Unlike web-design-guidelines",
              "text": "which runs the same kind of review on UI code"
            },
            {
              "label": "Needs",
              "text": "web access, since the rules are fetched fresh before each review"
            }
          ],
          "text": "The skill itself is a short wrapper: the rules live in Vercel's separate writing-guidelines repo, and the agent fetches them before every review, so they can change between runs. You name a file or pattern, or it asks which files. In October 2026 the rules covered planning each page and declaring its type (tutorial, how-to, reference and so on), active voice and addressing the reader as \"you\", banned words such as easy, simple and just, vague claims to replace with figures, and signs of AI-written prose such as recap transitions and choppy fragments. They also covered sentence-case headings, code blocks of at most 25 lines with a language tag, snake_case placeholders, units written as 64 KB, and no em dashes. Many rules are specific to Vercel's docs, such as its metadata fields and components, so it fits docs that follow Vercel's conventions best. It reports each issue and where it is, explaining only when the fix isn't obvious.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/vercel-labs/agent-skills/tree/063bee94c3f4df8453406c830b0a7df0f2860278/skills/writing-guidelines"
        },
        "tags": [
          "category:writing",
          "type:workflow",
          "effect:none",
          "needs:none",
          "by:official",
          "writing",
          "docs-writing",
          "code-review"
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "anthropics/skills/skills/frontend-design",
      "name": "frontend-design",
      "skills.sh": {
        "url": "https://skills.sh/anthropics/skills/frontend-design",
        "installs": 1084617,
        "weeklyInstalls": [22329, 22390, 23150, 24769, 25306, 27828, 29060, 30354],
        "firstSeen": "2026-01-19"
      },
      "skillMd": {
        "version": "dbd4588f9e1033efb41dad4bef2f7947c8993d44",
        "updated": "2026-10-20",
        "skew": 0
      },
      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Gives UI a distinctive aesthetic direction, typography and layout.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "you're building or reworking a web page or app UI and want it to look designed, not generated"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "plans colors, type and layout for your subject, checks the plan against common AI-design looks, then builds and critiques"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "the UI code, built from a short design plan: 4 to 6 named colors, typefaces, a layout sketch and principles"
            },
            {
              "label": "Avoids",
              "text": "defaults like cream backgrounds with terracotta accents, identical rounded cards, all-caps labels above headings, fade-ins on every section"
            },
            {
              "label": "Builds in",
              "text": "mobile layouts, visible keyboard focus, accessible contrast, and respect for users' reduced-motion setting"
            }
          ],
          "text": "Anthropic's design guidance. It asks the agent to act like a designer whose client has already rejected anything that looks templated. If you don't say what the product is, the agent proposes a subject, audience and main purpose and checks with you, since distinctive choices come from the subject's own world. It then writes a compact plan: four to six named hex colors, one or two typefaces with their roles, a layout concept with rough text wireframes, and guiding principles. Before coding, it compares the plan with looks that AI-generated design keeps producing, such as cream with a serif and terracotta, near-black with one acid-green accent, or a kit of identical rounded cards, and revises any part that's a default rather than a choice, unless your brief asked for it. Guidance covers typography (lines under 80 characters, no single highlighted word in a headline), numbered markers only for real sequences, one deliberate animation rather than scattered effects, and boldness spent in one place. A section on interface copy asks for plain, active, consistent words: \"Save changes\", not \"Submit\". Where it can, it screenshots its own work to critique it.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/anthropics/skills/tree/dbd4588f9e1033efb41dad4bef2f7947c8993d44/skills/frontend-design"
        },
        "tags": [
          "category:design",
          "type:guidelines",
          "effect:none",
          "needs:none",
          "by:official",
          "design",
          "anti-slop"
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "vercel-labs/agent-skills/skills/web-design-guidelines",
      "name": "web-design-guidelines",
      "skills.sh": {
        "url": "https://skills.sh/vercel-labs/agent-skills/web-design-guidelines",
        "installs": 832663,
        "weeklyInstalls": [18182, 20722, 21304, 26380, 26754, 30623, 30032, 28740],
        "firstSeen": "2026-01-19"
      },
      "skillMd": {
        "version": "063bee94c3f4df8453406c830b0a7df0f2860278",
