      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Reads the brief, picks a design direction and ships non-generic interfaces.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "you're building or redesigning a landing page, marketing site or portfolio, or a page looks generic and AI-made"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "has a script offer three design directions from a library built on 247 sites from galleries like Awwwards, then builds around an idea from your subject"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "a finished page with full copy, real credited pictures and motion, plus a report listing the direction, critique scores and sample content to swap before launch"
            },
            {
              "label": "Checks",
              "text": "the page in a real browser at three widths, failing headlines over two lines, gradients, shadows, small labels above headings and empty stretches"
            },
            {
              "label": "Unlike the fixed looks",
              "text": "high-end-visual-design and minimalist-ui, the same repo's opt-in skills that each apply one style, it picks a new direction per project and measures the result"
            },
            {
              "label": "Needs",
              "text": "Node 22+, and Chrome, Edge or Chromium; network access to search open image collections"
            }
          ],
          "text": "A page an agent builds by default looks like the average of every page it has seen; this skill replaces those defaults with decisions. The agent first lists eight to twelve things only this client has, such as its street, its numbers or its rituals, and plans the page as claims with proof, one per section. A script picks three art directions at random from its library; the agent chooses the one that suits the subject, and the script then supplies its type, palette, hero and section layouts. The page needs an idea from the subject that a competitor couldn't use, and one interactive element built from it. The agent writes the full copy, then builds with real pictures: the project's own, generated ones, or credited images from open collections such as Wikimedia Commons and the Met. A linter and a browser inspector enforce its style rules, such as no gradients, monospace text, numbered steps or pill-shaped labels. The agent then critiques the screenshots, fixes and checks again, adds motion and reports. Dashboards and other app screens get their own process and rules; redesigns are measured before and after.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/leonxlnx/taste-skill/tree/6010f91a9935f1c167dfc500fb1a475681892e6b/skills/taste-skill"
        },
        "tags": [
          "category:design",
          "type:workflow",
          "effect:local",
          "needs:browser",
          "by:community",
          "design",
          "anti-slop"
        ]
      }
