      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Searchable styles, palettes, font pairings and UX rules for web, mobile and desktop.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "you're designing, building or reviewing an interface and want concrete picks for style, colors, fonts and UX rules"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "runs a bundled Python search over local data: styles, palettes, font pairings, UX guidelines and advice per tech stack"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "a design system for your product (style, colors, typography, effects, things to avoid), optionally saved as a file in your project"
            },
            {
              "label": "Covers",
              "text": "22 stacks, from React and Next.js to SwiftUI, Flutter and WPF, plus GSAP animation presets and chart types"
            },
            {
              "label": "Needs",
              "text": "Python 3, with no other dependencies"
            },
            {
              "label": "Unlike",
              "text": "Anthropic's frontend-design, which has the agent make its own distinctive design choices, it picks from curated data and says when nothing matched"
            }
          ],
          "text": "UI/UX Pro Max packs design knowledge into searchable data files: 79 styles, 192 product palettes with the reasoning behind them, 74 font pairings, 119 UX guidelines, icons, animation presets and chart types. For a new page or project, the agent describes the product, say \"beauty spa wellness service\", and gets back a design system: a layout pattern, a style, colors, typography, effects and anti-patterns. Optional 1-10 settings make it bolder or plainer, add more or less motion, and space things out or pack them in for dashboards. Saved, it becomes one master design file for the project, plus optional files for pages that differ, and an existing master is never overwritten without your approval. For narrower questions it searches one area, such as UX rules, colors or your detected stack. Its rules are ranked, with accessibility and touch targets first and charts last. If a search comes back empty, it retries once, then says plainly that it's falling back to general defaults.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/nextlevelbuilder/ui-ux-pro-max-skill/tree/56ea9cf174ed86786187daa9de0e6247cb0747e4/.claude/skills/ui-ux-pro-max"
