      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Applies Emil Kowalski's approach to UI polish, components and animation.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "you're building or reviewing UI and want its animations, buttons and popovers to feel polished, not just work"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "applies design engineer Emil Kowalski's rules on whether to animate, which easing to use and how fast, plus component and gesture details"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "UI code with tuned motion; a review comes as one Before / After / Why table"
            },
            {
              "label": "Unlike review-animations",
              "text": "it helps you build as well as review, and isn't limited to motion code"
            }
          ],
          "text": "The main skill of Emil Kowalski's set, mostly about animation with some general UI advice. Before writing any animation, the agent answers four questions in order. Should this animate at all? Not for keyboard shortcuts, nor for anything people see a hundred times a day. What is it for? Which easing? Ease-out for things entering or leaving, never ease-in. How fast? Under 300ms for UI. Then it applies concrete rules: pressed buttons shrink slightly, nothing grows from zero size, popovers grow out of the button that opened them, CSS transitions beat keyframe animations for anything that can be interrupted, springs suit drags, and only transform and opacity are animated so motion stays smooth. It also covers swipe-to-dismiss, reduced-motion settings and lessons from building his widely used toast library. When it reviews UI code, it answers in a single Before / After / Why table, one row per issue.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/emilkowalski/skills/tree/2d228630ba1446365f614a108cb8252f1feda202/skills/emil-design-eng"
        },
        "tags": [
          "category:design",
          "type:guidelines",
          "effect:none",
          "needs:none",
          "by:community",
          "design",
          "animation"
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "emilkowalski/skills/skills/review-animations",
      "name": "review-animations",
      "skills.sh": {
        "url": "https://skills.sh/emilkowalski/skills/review-animations",
        "installs": 287170,
        "weeklyInstalls": [11920, 13772, 14870, 16376, 18107, 18273, 20074, 18236],
        "firstSeen": "2026-06-18"
      },
      "skillMd": {
        "version": "f1090bec277140d02ed202440185ad9d02ac1490",
        "updated": "2026-11-03",
        "skew": 19
      },
      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Reviews motion code against a high craft bar.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "you want animation or motion code checked against a strict bar before it ships"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "measures every animation in the change against ten standards, defaulting to flagging problems rather than approving"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "a Before / After / Why table of fixes, remaining notes ranked by impact, and a Block or Approve verdict"
            },
            {
              "label": "Unlike emil-design-eng",
              "text": "that skill helps build UI in general; this one only reviews motion code, and won't write features"
            },
            {
              "label": "Runs",
              "text": "only when you invoke it by name; the agent never starts it on its own"
            }
          ],
          "text": "A strict reviewer built on design engineer Emil Kowalski's animation rules, where approval has to be earned. Its ten standards ask whether each animation has a reason to exist, suits how often people see it, uses responsive easing, stays under 300ms, grows out of the element that triggered it, can be interrupted, animates only transform and opacity, respects reduced-motion settings, fits the product's personality, and lets deliberate actions like hold-to-delete move slowly while the release snaps back fast. Some patterns are flagged on sight, such as \"transition: all\", entrances from zero size, ease-in on UI, or animation on a keyboard shortcut. Fixes follow a preferred order: first delete the animation, then shorten it, then fix the easing, and only later polish. The review cites file and line, and ends with an explicit Block or Approve.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/emilkowalski/skills/tree/2d228630ba1446365f614a108cb8252f1feda202/skills/review-animations"
        },
        "tags": [
          "category:design",
