            }
          ],
          "text": "It works in two steps. First the agent writes a design philosophy, which gives the piece one coherent visual language: it names an imaginary art movement in a word or two, such as \"Concrete Poetry\" or \"Chromatic Silence\", and describes in four to six paragraphs how it uses space, color, scale and composition. Then it expresses that philosophy on a single page, as a PDF or PNG, working in your topic as a subtle nod rather than a literal illustration. The instructions push toward something like a diagram from an imaginary science: dense repeated marks, layered patterns, sparse clinical labels and a small, cohesive palette, with nothing overlapping or running off the edge. Fonts come from a bundled folder. A final pass refines what's there rather than adding more. On request it makes more pages, each a variation on the first, like a coffee-table book. It's told to make original work, never copying existing artists.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/anthropics/skills/tree/dbd4588f9e1033efb41dad4bef2f7947c8993d44/skills/canvas-design"
        },
        "tags": [
