      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Controls a browser over CDP for automation, scraping and testing.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "a task needs a real browser: clicking, typing, logged-in pages, JavaScript-heavy or bot-protected sites"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "lets the agent drive your running Chrome by piping short Python snippets to the browser-use command"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "the task done in background tabs without Chrome jumping to the front, with an optional recording of the session"
            },
            {
              "label": "Needs",
              "text": "the browser-use CLI and Chrome with remote debugging on; cloud browsers need a paid Browser Use account"
            },
            {
              "label": "Unlike agent-browser",
              "text": "another browser CLI where each action is a command, here the agent writes Python against ready-made helpers and raw DevTools calls"
            }
          ],
          "text": "It starts by saying when not to use it: if a plain fetch can read the page, use that. Otherwise the agent sends Python to browser-use, with helpers such as new_tab, page_info, click_at_xy and js already loaded, through a background process that stays connected to Chrome over the DevTools protocol between calls. It finds elements in the accessibility tree rather than in screenshots, clicks at their coordinates and checks the result. It keeps one tab per site, closes the tabs it opened, and stops at login walls unless you're already signed in through SSO. For several tasks at once, or sites likely to show captchas, it suggests cloud browsers from Browser Use, the company behind the tool: fresh, isolated, less likely to be blocked, and billed until stopped, so it asks before leaving one running. On macOS it can approve Chrome's remote-debugging prompt itself.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/browser-use/browser-use/tree/aa093a843da5edbf323c0b4145c9b6a0db3ecde8/skills/browser-use"
