      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Creates, reads and edits PowerPoint decks.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "a PowerPoint file is involved: making a deck, editing one, filling a template, or pulling text out of slides"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "creates decks with the pptxgenjs JavaScript library, and edits existing ones by unzipping them and changing the slide XML"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "a .pptx that passes its validator and has been rendered to images and checked by eye"
            },
            {
              "label": "Design rules",
              "text": "a palette chosen for the topic, a visual on every slide, varied layouts, no lines under titles or decorative stripes"
            },
            {
              "label": "Needs",
              "text": "Node, Python and LibreOffice, for building, checking and rendering slides"
            }
          ],
          "text": "Anthropic's PowerPoint skill. Much of it is a list of pitfalls that break decks: for instance, a # in a hex color or a negative shadow offset corrupts the file, and some chart settings make PowerPoint discard the chart. Charts stay native, not pasted images. To edit a deck or fill a template, the agent unzips the file, maps your content onto the template's slides with a labelled thumbnail grid, adds and removes slides with bundled scripts before touching any text, then edits the XML and zips it back. Design guidance asks for one dominant color with a single contrasting one, a repeated visual motif, big number callouts, fonts that render reliably, and none of the usual signs of AI-made slides, such as text-only slides, centered body text or cream backgrounds. Every deck goes through checks: a search for leftover placeholder text, a validator that names the fix for each fault, and a render to images the agent inspects for overflowing, overlapping or cramped elements, ideally with a subagent's fresh eyes.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/anthropics/skills/tree/dbd4588f9e1033efb41dad4bef2f7947c8993d44/skills/pptx"
        },
