      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Reads, merges, splits, fills and OCRs PDFs.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "you need to do something with a PDF: pull out text or tables, merge, split, fill a form, or create one"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "picks the right Python library or command-line tool for the task and runs it"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "the extracted text or tables, or a new PDF: merged, split, rotated, watermarked, password-protected or filled in"
            },
            {
              "label": "Forms",
              "text": "fills a form's own fields when it has them, or writes text in the right places on a form that has none"
            },
            {
              "label": "Scanned PDFs",
              "text": "turns pages into images and reads them with OCR"
            },
            {
              "label": "Needs",
              "text": "Python PDF libraries such as pypdf, plus common command-line PDF tools"
            }
          ],
          "text": "Mostly ready-to-use code for common PDF jobs. Python libraries handle merging, splitting, rotating, watermarking, metadata and passwords; extracting text with its layout, and tables that can go straight into a spreadsheet; and creating new PDFs, from a single page to multi-page reports. Command-line tools cover the same ground, plus pulling out embedded images. Scanned pages are converted to images and read with OCR. Filling a form has its own strict procedure in a separate file. The agent first checks whether the PDF has fillable fields. If it does, it lists them, renders the pages as images to work out what each field means, writes the values to a JSON file and fills them with a script that validates them. If not, it adds text at the right coordinates, read from the PDF's structure when possible and estimated from the page images otherwise. A further reference covers JavaScript libraries and more advanced command-line options.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/anthropics/skills/tree/dbd4588f9e1033efb41dad4bef2f7947c8993d44/skills/pdf"
        },
