      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Creates and edits spreadsheets with formulas and formatting.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "a spreadsheet is the deliverable: building one, fixing one, or turning messy data into one"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "writes Excel files with openpyxl, using live formulas instead of computed numbers, then recalculates them in LibreOffice to catch errors"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "an .xlsx with no formula errors and its assumptions labeled; a file you gave it keeps its own conventions"
            },
            {
              "label": "Avoids",
              "text": "newer functions such as XLOOKUP or FILTER, which that LibreOffice check can't evaluate, using INDEX and MATCH instead"
            },
            {
              "label": "Needs",
              "text": "Python with openpyxl and pandas, plus LibreOffice"
            }
          ],
          "text": "Anthropic's skill for spreadsheets (.xlsx, .xlsm, .csv, .tsv). Outputs must use real formulas, so the sheet updates when its inputs change, and must ship with no formula errors. Because openpyxl saves formulas without their results, a bundled script recalculates the workbook in LibreOffice and names every error by cell; the agent fixes them and reruns until it's clean. A clean run only proves formulas evaluate, not that they're right, so the agent checks its first few formulas by hand before filling a grid. The skill also lists ways openpyxl silently damages files: loading only the cached values and then saving turns every formula into a fixed number, macros vanish unless kept explicitly, and links to other workbooks are lost. When editing your file, the agent writes only to its input cells and leaves its formulas alone. A workbook it builds for others to fill in gets a legend and one example row, and every hardcoded number gets a visible note or source. Financial models follow standard formatting conventions unless you say otherwise.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/anthropics/skills/tree/dbd4588f9e1033efb41dad4bef2f7947c8993d44/skills/xlsx"
        },
