      "skillMd": {
        "version": "0cd9b3a0ef4c411a1d690419ce843f2a1da32cce",
        "updated": "2026-10-28",
        "skew": 11
      },
      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Writes homepage, landing, pricing and feature-page copy.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "you need copy for a homepage, landing, pricing, feature or about page, or a draft sounds like AI"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "first decides whether to write from scratch, restore the voice of your draft, or change only the line you asked about"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "page copy by section, notes on why each choice was made, and 2-3 options for headlines and buttons"
            },
            {
              "label": "Bans",
              "text": "telltale AI phrasing such as \"it's not X, it's Y\", strings of \"no X, no Y\", and openers like \"In today's fast-paced world\""
            },
            {
              "label": "Won't",
              "text": "invent stats or proof: gaps are marked [NEED: ...], and a refused claim says what evidence would allow it"
            }
          ],
          "text": "Marketing copy for web pages, written the way a careful senior copywriter would. It asks about the page's one goal, the audience, the offer and where visitors come from, and reads your product marketing context if you have it (a shared file on your product, audience and positioning that the repo's product-marketing skill writes). Then it says which of three cases it's in. Given a blank brief or generic copy, it builds the argument but never invents facts. Given a draft with a real voice, it keeps your words and angle and fixes what's weak. Given a point edit, it changes only that line and returns just the changed lines with a reason each. It writes the page in pieces (headline, subhead, button, each section), drafting two or three versions of each and keeping the best. Every line must pass three questions: can the reader picture it, can it be proven true or false, and could a competitor write the same sentence? Most lines stay plain, with one or two punchy moments per page, and before handing over it searches the draft for a list of banned AI patterns. For line-by-line polish of existing copy, the repo has copy-editing.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/coreyhaines31/marketingskills/tree/e85f3cbc2e4a79bf33b2a12e9bdd9087d414735b/skills/copywriting"
        },
