      "id": "coreyhaines31/marketingskills/skills/marketing-psychology",
      "name": "marketing-psychology",
      "skills.sh": {
        "url": "https://skills.sh/coreyhaines31/marketingskills/marketing-psychology",
        "installs": 172566,
        "weeklyInstalls": [3350, 3152, 3246, 3877, 4035, 3939, 4342, 4755],
        "firstSeen": "2026-01-19"
      },
      "skillMd": {
        "version": "e85f3cbc2e4a79bf33b2a12e9bdd9087d414735b",
        "updated": "2026-10-20",
        "skew": 0
      },
      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Applies behavioral science to marketing.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "you want to know why people buy, or which bias or mental model fits a pricing, conversion or retention problem"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "picks the mental models that apply, explains the psychology behind each, and suggests ethical ways to use them"
            },
            {
              "label": "Covers",
              "text": "72 models, from first-principles thinking to anchoring, loss aversion, social proof and network effects"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "advice in the chat, not finished copy or pages"
            },
            {
              "label": "Unlike",
              "text": "copywriting from the same repo, it recommends tactics and explains them rather than writing the words"
            }
          ],
          "text": "A reference of mental models and behavioral science for marketing decisions, in six groups: thinking tools for strategy (inversion, the 80/20 rule, finding the one bottleneck, second-order effects), how buyers decide (we value what we own, fear change, freeze with too many options), persuasion (reciprocity, scarcity, anchoring, loss aversion, framing), pricing (prices ending in 9, when \"20% off\" beats \"$50 off\", three tiers with the middle as the target), designing for action (fewer choices decide faster; behavior needs motivation, ability and a prompt) and growth (flywheels, network effects, switching costs). Each model comes with a one-line marketing example. A quick-reference table maps common problems to models: low conversions, price objections, building trust, churn, stalled growth. It asks what behavior you want to change and what currently stops it, and insists on using tactics such as scarcity only when they're genuine.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/coreyhaines31/marketingskills/tree/e85f3cbc2e4a79bf33b2a12e9bdd9087d414735b/skills/marketing-psychology"
        },
        "tags": [
          "category:marketing",
          "type:guidelines",
          "effect:none",
          "needs:none",
          "by:community",
          "marketing",
          "conversion"
        ]
      }
    },
