              "text": "a report with the score, a profit breakdown, entry costs, risks and a recommendation to pursue or avoid"
            },
            {
              "label": "Limits",
              "text": "it works from public web data, so sales and costs are estimates; for live data it points to its maker Nexscope's paid analytics"
            },
            {
              "label": "Covers",
              "text": "12 Amazon marketplaces, including the US, UK, Germany, Japan, India and Brazil"
            },
            {
              "label": "Unlike",
              "text": "cross-border-ecommerce, also by Nexscope, which scores countries to expand into rather than products to sell"
            }
          ],
          "text": "A research framework for Amazon sellers from Nexscope, an e-commerce analytics company. Given a product idea, such as yoga mats or a $3 AliExpress find selling for $25 on Amazon, the agent runs web searches in five steps: search interest and seasonality; competition, meaning how many sellers there are, which brands dominate and how reviews and prices spread; demand signals from Google Trends, related searches and Reddit; profit, from Alibaba wholesale prices and Amazon's selling and fulfilment fees; and barriers to entry such as startup capital, minimum order quantities and certifications. Eight factors, each scored 1 to 10 from a fixed rubric, combine into the overall score: demand counts 25%, competition and profit 20% each, entry difficulty 15%, and growth, differentiation, seasonality and risk the rest. The report covers the market, the competition, a cost-by-cost profit estimate, what entry takes, risks, and a strategy that depends on the score: go ahead, go ahead with changes, or avoid and consider alternatives. Several products can be compared in one table.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/nexscope-ai/amazon-skills/tree/0f3b13fa0e5ed0a9f3d600dc18518bc76ddd813b/amazon-product-research"
        },
