        "skew": 0
      },
      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Prepares a conda environment, datasets and checkpoints for a reproduction.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "a reproduction target is chosen and the environment, datasets and model checkpoints must be ready before the first run"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "plans a conda environment from the README and repo files, and works out where checkpoints, datasets and caches should go"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "setup notes, candidate conda commands, an asset list with sources, paths and status, and the risks still open"
            },
            {
              "label": "Comes between",
              "text": "RigorPilot's repo-mapping step (repo-intake-and-plan) and its first run (minimal-run-and-audit)"
            }
          ],
          "text": "The setup step in RigorPilot, a set of skills that reproduces deep-learning research code by following its README. For the environment it trusts, in order, the README's instructions, the repo's environment files, package metadata, then careful guesses from imports. It reuses an existing environment.yml, otherwise translates the requirements into a simple conda-plus-pip setup, avoids aggressive upgrades and records version doubts. A script that comes with the skill can create the environment, or just print the commands. For assets it finds checkpoints, datasets and caches through README links, configs and code, sticks to documented sources, and records each one's source, local path and status: present, missing, downloaded, skipped or unknown. It targets Linux first and supports Windows and macOS where practical; containers, cluster schedulers and custom CUDA builds are out of scope.",
