            }
          ],
          "text": "The exploration side of RigorPilot, a set of skills that reproduces deep-learning research code by following its README, kept separate from its reproduction work. You fix the ground rules first: task, dataset, benchmark, how results are evaluated, the state-of-the-art results you compare against, and a compute budget. The agent then alternates between planning and trying. While planning, it studies the repo, keeps your own ideas, adds a few single-change ideas of its own, looks up supporting papers (in a local reference manager such as Zotero first, the web optionally) and ranks ideas by expected gain, cost, risk and ease of rollback. While trying, it makes one bounded change or run, checks it quickly, collects evidence and ranks it against the baseline; measured results outrank predictions. It stops for you at blockers, unclear scientific meaning, an exhausted budget or ideas too close to call. It won't call a gain new or state-of-the-art without a literature comparison, ablations and a fair comparison.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/lllllllama/rigorpilot-skills/tree/fb3ccdf5aa64b1fa5b1d0463e666a8bc8bc26e01/skills/ai-research-explore"
        },
